window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["yj"]=[{"id":"front-bumper-yj","title":"Steel Front Bumper for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":320,"high":800},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","breaker bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["armor","bumper","front","steel","winch","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt SRC Front Bumper (YJ)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Rough Country Front Bumper (YJ)","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Poison Spyder BFH Front Bumper (YJ-spec)","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":750},{"name":"Rugged Ridge HD Front Bumper (YJ)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":500}],"citations":[{"title":"Smittybilt SRC front bumper \u2014 quadratec.com","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/smittybilt/src-front-bumper-yj"},{"title":"Poison Spyder YJ bumper","url":"https://www.poisonspyder.com/"}],"alternatives":["skid-plates-yj","winch-yj","rock-sliders-yj"],"summary":"The YJ's front frame mounting points are a different design than the TJ and LJ \u2014 the frame horn geometry and bolt pattern changed when Chrysler redesigned the Wrangler for the 1997 TJ. A TJ-specific bumper will not fit a YJ without modification. Always confirm the bumper is listed as YJ-compatible before purchasing.","description":"The factory YJ front bumper is thin steel with no recovery functionality. A steel aftermarket bumper is among the most useful first upgrades: it adds D-ring attachment points, eliminates the risk of damage to the factory tow hooks under recovery loads, and provides a winch platform. The YJ's electrical system limits apply to winch selection, covered separately.\n\nThe Smittybilt SRC YJ front bumper is the budget benchmark. It includes integrated D-ring mounts, a center winch plate, and bolt-in installation using the YJ's existing frame horn locations. Quality is acceptable; the weld finish is basic and the holes require some fitting. The Rough Country unit is similar in price and construction. Both have winch plates that accept standard winch footprints.\n\nThe Rugged Ridge HD bumper is the value mid-tier option: better steel gauge, tighter fit, and integrated light tabs. It's YJ-specific and bolt-in. Poison Spyder's BFH is the best mid-tier option for trail use \u2014 it's thick steel, purpose-designed for wheeling, and the approach angle geometry is better than the cheaper alternatives. At $750 it's the right choice for a YJ that does real rock work.\n\nThe 4.2L YJ (1987\u20131990) has a 60\u201370 amp alternator that is marginal for sustained winch use without an alternator upgrade. The 4.0L YJ (1991\u20131995) has a 90-amp alternator and can support a 9,500\u201312,000 lb winch with the same limitations as other 4.0L Wranglers. If you're running an early 4.2L YJ, plan the alternator upgrade before installing a winch \u2014 see the winch-wiring-yj entry for details.\n\nBefore ordering, confirm the bumper specifies YJ compatibility \u2014 not just \"Wrangler\" or \"TJ/LJ.\" Some budget vendors list broad compatibility that includes TJ geometry. A bumper that doesn't bolt to YJ frame horns requires cutting and welding to make work, which costs more in time than the budget savings."},{"id":"rear-bumper-yj","title":"Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":750},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","breaker bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["armor","bumper","rear","tire-carrier","swing-out","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt SRC Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier (YJ)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 YJ Rear Bumper","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":600},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Rear Bumper (YJ)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":420}],"citations":[{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 YJ bumpers","url":"https://rockhard4x4.com/"},{"title":"Smittybilt SRC YJ rear bumper","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/smittybilt/src-rear-bumper-yj"}],"alternatives":["front-bumper-yj","skid-plates-yj"],"summary":"The YJ's rear crossmember is a robust mounting point for an aftermarket bumper, and the factory rear bumper offers nothing for trail recovery. A steel rear bumper with D-ring mounts and a swing-out spare tire carrier adds recovery functionality, protects the rear of the tub, and handles a proper spare \u2014 all in a single bolt-in upgrade.","description":"The rear bumper is also where the YJ's spare tire ends up once you move to a 33-inch tire. The factory tailgate-mount spare carrier is not designed for tires larger than 30 inches and will flex and wear the tailgate hinges. Moving the spare to a swing-out carrier on the rear bumper is the right move at any tire size above stock.\n\nRock Hard 4x4 makes YJ-specific rear bumpers and is the most consistently praised option for quality and fit. The steel gauge is better than Smittybilt, the weld quality shows, and the swing-out arm uses a larger pin diameter than cheaper alternatives. At $600, it's the best value on the YJ where budget tier parts require more fitting work.\n\nThe Smittybilt SRC rear bumper for the YJ is available and works. Swing-out arm quality is acceptable; plan to apply thread locker to the hinge hardware after the first season. D-ring mounts are included and properly welded. At $450, it's the budget-friendly option for a YJ that doesn't see aggressive trail use regularly.\n\nThe swing-out arm must be rated for the tire you're running. A 33-inch tire on a steel wheel weighs 60\u201375 lbs. Verify the stated tire size capacity before buying. Any of the bumpers listed here are rated for a 33-inch spare. The factory tailgate-mount spare is not appropriate for a 33.\n\nThe YJ's factory rear tow loop is a safety concern under recovery load \u2014 it's not rated for kinetic recovery forces. With a steel bumper installed, the integrated D-rings are the correct attachment points. Front and rear rated recovery points are a prerequisite for safe self-recovery."},{"id":"rock-sliders-yj","title":"Rock Sliders and Rocker Guards for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"armor","subcategory":"sliders","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":700},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","drill","grinder (for DIY)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","frame identification"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["armor","rock-sliders","rockers","frame-mounted","protection","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"Poison Spyder Rock Sliders (YJ)","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":600},{"name":"Genright Rock Sliders (YJ)","vendor":"Genright","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"TNT Customs Rock Sliders (YJ)","vendor":"TNT Customs","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"DIY weld-on tube sliders (2\" DOM, steel)","vendor":"DIY","price_estimate_usd":150}],"citations":[{"title":"Poison Spyder YJ rock sliders","url":"https://www.poisonspyder.com/"},{"title":"TNT Customs YJ sliders","url":"https://www.tntcustoms.com/"}],"alternatives":["skid-plates-yj","front-bumper-yj"],"summary":"The YJ's rockers are more exposed than they look. The tub sides sit relatively low and in some spots the body metal actually dips below the top of the frame rail \u2014 which means a rock contact hits the body before anything structural. Frame-mounted rock sliders protect the body by transferring the load to the frame before the tub takes a hit.","description":"The YJ's aftermarket for sliders is smaller than the TJ/JK market, but the main trail-oriented brands all make YJ-specific sliders. The critical point remains the same across all platforms: frame-mounted tube sliders only. Body-mounted step sliders are decorative.\n\nBoth Poison Spyder and Genright make YJ rock sliders that mount to the frame outriggers. They're 2-inch DOM tube, properly welded, and designed to slide over rocks without catching. The installation uses the factory frame mounting provisions with some drilling required. At $600\u2013650, these are the quality picks for a YJ that does regular rock work.\n\nTNT Customs is a respected small-batch fabricator that makes YJ-specific sliders. At $450 they're a mid-price option with quality construction. Worth checking availability since small fabricators have varying production schedules.\n\nIf you have access to a welder and can source 2-inch DOM tube, a DIY weld-on slider set costs $150\u2013200 in steel. The trade-off is fabrication time (4\u20138 hours including fitting) and the quality of the mount brackets. A properly fabricated weld-on slider is as strong as a bolt-in commercial unit; a poorly fitted one is not. If you're comfortable with basic fabrication, DIY is the right budget call.\n\nThe YJ's unibody-adjacent tub construction means body hits that would just be cosmetic on a body-on-frame truck are structural on the Jeep. A dented or cracked tub floor is expensive to repair. Frame-mounted sliders take the rock's energy into the frame where it can handle it; the body stays intact."},{"id":"skid-plates-yj","title":"Skid Plate Kit for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"armor","subcategory":"skids","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":400},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","safe vehicle support"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["armor","skid-plates","protection","gas-tank","transfer-case","oil-pan","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"Skid Row Offroad YJ Full Skid Kit","vendor":"Skid Row Offroad","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 YJ Skid Plate System","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Rough Country 2-piece Skid Plate (YJ)","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":200}],"citations":[{"title":"Skid Row Offroad YJ kits","url":"https://www.skidrowoffroad.com/"},{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 YJ skid plates","url":"https://rockhard4x4.com/"}],"alternatives":["rock-sliders-yj","front-bumper-yj"],"summary":"The YJ's drivetrain is exposed: the transfer case and oil pan hang within easy striking distance of rocks on any trail with meaningful obstacles. The leaf-spring suspension geometry actually leaves the drivetrain slightly more exposed in some positions than the coil-sprung TJ, and the gas tank is also relatively unprotected from below. Skid plates are not an upgrade on a trail YJ \u2014 they're basic equipment.","description":"TJ skid kits are not YJ-compatible in most cases. The mounting points, transfer case position, and gas tank geometry are different between the leaf-sprung YJ and the coil-sprung TJ. Always confirm the skid kit specifies YJ compatibility.\n\nThe oil pan is the most exposed and most critical component. A single hard rock contact can crack it and strand you. Install an oil pan skid first, before any other armor. The Rough Country 2-piece kit covers the oil pan and transfer case at the budget end.\n\nThe YJ's NP231 (later years) or NP207 transfer case hangs behind the transmission and below the floor. It's in a vulnerable position on any terrain with rocks larger than a fist. A 3/16-inch steel skid wrapping the transfer case is the fix. Any full kit includes this.\n\nThe gas tank is vulnerable on the YJ \u2014 it sits behind the rear axle and the factory protection is minimal. A gas tank skid is especially important if you're running any lift, which increases belly exposure. The Skid Row kit covers all three priorities.\n\nRough Country's 2-piece budget kit covers the oil pan and transfer case and is adequate for mild trail use. The material is thinner than Rock Hard or Skid Row, but it's a significant improvement over no protection. Rock Hard 4x4 is a step up in steel thickness and fitment quality. Skid Row Offroad's YJ kit is the most comprehensive, covering oil pan, TC, and gas tank with good-gauge material. For a trail YJ, Rock Hard or Skid Row is the right call; save the Rough Country kit for a budget-first build."},{"id":"yj-dana-30-front","title":"Dana 30 Front Axle \u2014 Service and Upgrade Path (YJ)","vehicle":"yj","category":"axles","subcategory":"front_axle","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":1500},"tools_required":["floor jack + jack stands","ball-joint press","snap-ring pliers","torque wrench","U-joint press / large socket set","pry bar","grease"],"skills_required":["front-axle service (U-joints, ball joints)","hub/unit-bearing R&R"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["axles","dana-30","front-axle","u-joint","ball-joint","gears"],"parts":[{"name":"Front axle U-joints (pair, non-greaseable Spicer)","vendor":"Spicer / Dana","price_estimate_usd":50},{"name":"Ball joint set (upper + lower, both sides)","vendor":"Moog / Spicer","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Inner axle seals + unit bearing (per side)","vendor":"Timken / National","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Ring & pinion + install kit (regear path)","vendor":"Yukon / Nitro","price_estimate_usd":350}],"citations":[{"title":"Spicer/Dana 30 axle service specifications (Jeep front)","url":"https://www.spicerparts.com/"},{"title":"Jeep YJ FSM \u2014 front axle U-joint and ball joint service","url":"https://www.jeep.com/"}],"alternatives":["yj-dana-35-rear-upgrade","yj-steering-wander-wobble"],"summary":"The Dana 30 front under a YJ is a capable, well-supported axle that handles 31s easily and 33s with care. Most \"front axle\" problems are wear items: clicking U-joints, sloppy ball joints, and leaking seals \u2014 all serviceable in a driveway. The Dana 30 only becomes the weak link past 35-inch tires or hard rock use, at which point you regear, add a locker, and upgrade shafts rather than swap the housing. Budget $60 for a U-joint, up to ~$1,500 if you regear and lock it.","description":"The high-pinion Dana 30 is the front axle in every YJ. It is not a heavy-duty axle, but it is plentiful, cheap to service, and strong enough for the tire sizes most YJs run. The key is to treat the wear items before they cause trouble: a worn front U-joint or ball joint shows up as steering wander, clunking, and uneven tire wear, and a neglected one can leave you stranded with a locked-up front hub.\n\nA **clicking sound on full lock** is the classic worn front-axle U-joint \u2014 the closed-knuckle Dana 30 uses a U-joint inside the knuckle as the steering/drive joint. Replace them in pairs with non-greaseable Spicer joints (the solid caps last longer than greaseable ones in this application). **Ball joints** wear and cause wander and death-wobble contributions; check for play with the tire off the ground and replace upper and lower as a set, then get an alignment. **Inner axle seals** weep gear oil into the inner knuckle \u2014 replace the seal and the unit/hub bearing together while you are in there.\n\nFor 31s and most 33-inch builds, a healthy Dana 30 with good joints is plenty. It starts to show its limits past 33s, under repeated hard rock impacts, or when paired with a front locker that puts full torque through both shafts. The upgrade path, in order of value: regear to match your tires (4.56 for 33s), add a selectable locker or limited-slip, and step up to chromoly axle shafts and U-joints if you break stock parts. A full housing swap (Dana 44 or aftermarket) is the last step and only worth it on a serious 37-inch build.\n\nPull the wheel, caliper, and rotor/hub, then the unit bearing and stub shaft. The U-joint comes out of the inner axle with a press or a vise and sockets \u2014 support the yoke ears so you do not crack them. New ball joints want a ball-joint press and proper torque. Reassemble with fresh seals, set hub bearing preload per the unit-bearing spec, refill the differential, and align the front end afterward.","pros":["Cheap, plentiful, and fully serviceable in a home driveway","Strong enough for 31s and most 33-inch YJ builds","Clear upgrade path (gears, locker, shafts) before any housing swap"],"cons":["Closed-knuckle U-joints and ball joints are real wear items \u2014 inspect them","Becomes the weak link past 35s or under a hard front locker","A neglected front U-joint can fail and lock the hub on the trail"]},{"id":"yj-dana-35-rear-upgrade","title":"The Dana 35 Rear: Live With It or Upgrade","vehicle":"yj","category":"axles","subcategory":"rear_axle","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":20},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","axle shaft puller","bearing tools","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["axle work","gear setup (or a shop)"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["axles","drivetrain","regear"],"parts":[{"name":"Dana 35 chromoly axle shaft set","vendor":"Yukon/RCV","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Ring and pinion (4.10/4.56)","vendor":"Yukon/Motive","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Locker or Trac-Lok","vendor":"Eaton/ARB/Yukon Grizzly","price_estimate_usd":450}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep axle reference \u2014 List of Jeep axles","url":"https://jeep.fandom.com/wiki/List_of_Jeep_axles"},{"title":"Yukon Dana 35 upgrade catalog","url":"https://yukongear.com/"}],"alternatives":["yj-lift-kit-guide"],"summary":"Every YJ left the factory with a Dana 35 rear, and it is the platform's known weak link. On 31s with a daily-driven truck it is fine. On 33s with a locker and a heavy right foot, the c-clip shafts snap and the tubes bend. Whether you upgrade depends honestly on how you use the truck.","description":"The straight answer: if you run 31\" tires, drive the YJ on the street and mild trails, and do not have a rear locker, the Dana 35 will live \u2014 keep it, and put your money into springs and steering. The failure mode is predictable: it breaks under shock load with traction (a locked rear on 33s climbing a ledge), and because it uses c-clip axle shafts, a broken shaft can leave the wheel and tire behind you on the trail.\n\nIf you are building past 33s, locking the rear, or wheeling hard, the Dana 35 becomes a liability and the question is repair-tier versus replace-tier. Repair-tier: chromoly or RCV shafts and a c-clip eliminator address the shaft failure and the wheel-loss risk for a few hundred dollars, and a Grizzly/Detroit locker handles traction. That keeps a 35 alive for a moderate 33\" build. Replace-tier: swap to a Ford 8.8 (cheap, strong, disc brakes, same width family with the right shafts) or a Dana 44 \u2014 the real answer for a 35\"+ rock truck.\n\nWhile the axle is apart for any of this, regear both ends to match your tires \u2014 4.10 for 31s, 4.56 for 33s on the 4.0L. Regearing only the rear is never an option; the front Dana 30 must match exactly.","steps":["Identify your rear: all factory YJ rears are Dana 35 c-clip \u2014 confirm the cover shape and ratio tag.","For a keep-it build: install chromoly shafts + a c-clip eliminator and a locker.","For a replace build: source a Ford 8.8 or Dana 44 of the correct width and shaft pattern.","Regear front and rear to the same ratio for your tire size.","Have a shop set up the gears unless you own the tools and a dial indicator \u2014 gear setup is unforgiving."]},{"id":"yj-tub-rust","title":"YJ Tub and Footwell Rust Repair","vehicle":"yj","category":"body","subcategory":"rust-repair","type":"procedure","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":40},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":1500},"tools_required":["angle grinder + cutoff wheel","MIG welder","sheet-metal shears","wire wheel","rust converter","seam sealer","primer + paint"],"skills_required":["sheet-metal patch welding","panel fabrication or fitment"],"build_tiers":["stock","daily","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["body","rust","tub","footwell","floor-pan","welding"],"parts":[{"name":"Front floor pan / footwell patch panels (pair)","vendor":"Key Parts / Crown","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Rear corner / wheel-arch patch panels","vendor":"Key Parts","price_estimate_usd":140},{"name":"Weld-through primer, seam sealer, undercoating","vendor":"SEM / 3M","price_estimate_usd":80}],"citations":[{"title":"Key Parts / Classic 2 Current \u2014 YJ floor and body panel catalog","url":"https://www.classic2current.com/"},{"title":"Jeep YJ rust-repair writeups (footwell and rear corners)","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/"}],"alternatives":["yj-interior-refresh","yj-budget-trail"],"summary":"Every old YJ rusts in the same places: the front footwells under the pedals, the rear floor by the wheel wells, the seat-belt mounts, and the lower rear corners. Surface rust you can treat and seal; perforated metal at the footwells or seat-belt mounts is a safety and structural repair that needs cut-and-weld patch panels. Budget $60 for a localized seal-up, up to $1,500 in panels and consumables for a full floor. Do not bondo over rusted-through footwells \u2014 water gets in through the windshield and top, and a soft floor near the seat-belt anchors is dangerous.","description":"YJs are open-top Jeeps that live outside and take in water through the windshield seal, the soft-top seams, and the body drains. That water pools in the footwells and rear floor and rusts the tub from the inside out. The good news is that the YJ tub is bolted to the frame and reproduction panels are widely available, so even a badly rusted floor is repairable without replacing the whole body.\n\nPull the carpet, seats, and seat belts and inspect with a screwdriver or wire wheel. Press on the footwells, the floor under the seat-belt anchors, the rear floor by the wheel arches, and the lower body corners \u2014 anything that flexes or flakes through is a patch job. Common YJ rust zones are the front driver and passenger footwells, the rear floor/wheel-well seams, the seat-belt and seat-mount areas, and the rocker/lower corners. Map every spot now so you cut once.\n\n**Surface rust** that has not gone through gets wire-wheeled to clean metal, treated with a rust converter, primed, and sealed. **Perforated metal** \u2014 especially at footwells and seat-belt mounts \u2014 must be cut out and replaced with a welded patch panel; sealant or fiberglass over a structural hole is not a repair. Reproduction floor pans, footwell patches, and rear corners are available and largely pre-formed, which makes the welding straightforward if you can run a clean MIG bead on thin metal.\n\nCut back to solid metal, fit the patch with weld-through primer on the mating flanges, and stitch-weld to avoid warping the thin sheet. Grind, seam-seal every joint, prime, and paint. Undercoat the underside and treat the inside of the tub so the repair lasts. Reinstall with fresh body-mount bushings if they are crushed, and make sure the body drains are clear so water leaves instead of pooling again. Seat-belt anchor areas must be welded to solid structure \u2014 this is a safety-critical detail, not cosmetic.","pros":["Reproduction panels make even a bad YJ floor fully repairable","The tub bolts off the frame, so access for welding is good","Fixing it right protects the seat-belt mounts and resale value"],"cons":["Perforated footwells need welding skill, not filler","It is dirty, time-consuming work that often grows once you open it up","Skipping the drains and sealing means the rust comes right back"]},{"id":"yj-front-pads-rotors","title":"Front Brake Pad and Rotor Replacement","vehicle":"yj","category":"brakes","subcategory":"front_brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":220},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","lug wrench","C-clamp or caliper compressor","13mm and 17mm sockets","torque wrench","wire brush","brake cleaner"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","torque-spec discipline"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["brakes","safety-critical","wear-items"],"parts":[{"name":"PowerStop Z16 Autospecialty front kit (pads + rotors)","part_number":"KOE1003","vendor":"PowerStop","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Bosch QuietCast ceramic pads (front)","part_number":"BC228","vendor":"Bosch","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Raybestos Professional Grade rotors (pair)","part_number":"9961R","vendor":"Raybestos","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Brake cleaner aerosol","vendor":"CRC or Permatex","price_estimate_usd":5},{"name":"Anti-seize for caliper slide pins","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"PowerStop Z16 brake kit application \u2014 91-99 YJ/TJ","url":"https://www.powerstop.com/product/jeep-big-brakes/"},{"title":"Quadratec YJ brake drums and rotors catalog","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/vehicle/1987-1995-wrangler-yj/steering-and-brakes/brake-drums-and-rotors"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Pop the wheel, pull the two 13mm slide bolts, lift the caliper out of the bracket, swap pads, compress the piston with a C-clamp, then unbolt the bracket from the knuckle, slide the rotor off the hub, and reverse \u2014 about 90 minutes for both fronts on a YJ.","description":"Front brakes on a YJ are stopped-clock straightforward: single-piston floating caliper, vented rotor, two pad shims, two slide pins. The 1987\u20131989 trucks use a smaller rotor and caliper than the 1990+ trucks, which got the heavier brake setup that ran through every TJ and most XJs. Pads and rotors are wear items \u2014 replace pads when there's 3mm of friction material left, replace rotors any time you replace pads if the rotor is below minimum thickness (stamped on the hub face) or has hard scoring you can feel with a fingernail.\n\nTreat this job with respect \u2014 brakes are not where you cut corners or hurry. Use jack stands on the frame rails, not on the axle (the axle moves up when you remove the wheel and weight). Pump the brake pedal once after reassembly and before you move the truck to push the pads against the rotor; if you skip this, your first stop has no brakes. Then drive 10\u201315 mph in an empty lot and do a few firm stops to bed the new pads \u2014 this transfers a thin layer of friction material onto the rotor face, which is what actually does the stopping.\n\nCompressing the piston pushes brake fluid backward through the line and up into the master cylinder reservoir. Open the reservoir cap and remove a few ounces of fluid before you compress, or it'll overflow when you push the piston in. A C-clamp with the old pad as a pad-saver works fine; a proper caliper compressor tool is faster but $25 you don't need to spend. While the rotor is off, take 60 seconds with a wire brush to clean the hub face \u2014 rust between the hub and the rotor is what causes that pulsating brake pedal six months later.\n\nBrand math: PowerStop's Z16 kit at around $120 gets you pads and rotors that exceed factory performance and run cooler than the OEM Mopar units. Bosch QuietCast ceramic pads on Raybestos Professional rotors is the spend-no-more-than-needed option at about $115. Akebono ceramic pads are the quietest available but cost more \u2014 fair tradeoff if you daily-drive the YJ. Avoid the no-name $30 rotor kits on eBay; they warp inside a year and the pad material doesn't bed properly.","steps":["Loosen lug nuts while wheel is on the ground.","Jack up the front and set on jack stands under the frame rail forward of the leaf spring eye.","Remove the wheel.","Remove brake fluid from master cylinder reservoir (suck out ~1/3 with a turkey baster).","Remove the two 13mm caliper slide bolts on the back of the caliper.","Lift caliper off the bracket; hang it from the spring with wire \u2014 DO NOT let it dangle from the hose.","Pull old pads from the bracket (note position of any wear-indicator clip \u2014 it goes on the inboard pad).","Compress the piston with a C-clamp and the old inboard pad as a backer.","Wire-brush the bracket where pads slide; apply thin film of anti-seize to slide pins and pad-contact points on the bracket.","Install new pads. Apply anti-seize to slide bolts; reinstall caliper; torque slide bolts to 25 ft-lbs.","To replace rotors: remove the two 17mm bracket-to-knuckle bolts; lift the bracket off; slide rotor off the hub.","Wire-brush the hub face clean. Install new rotor.","Reinstall bracket; torque bracket bolts to 100 ft-lbs.","Repeat opposite side.","Refill master cylinder to MAX line. Pump pedal 5\u201310 times until firm.","Reinstall wheels, torque lug nuts to 80 ft-lbs in star pattern.","Drive at 10\u201315 mph and perform 5 firm (not panic) stops from 30 mph to bed pads."],"pros":["Standard floating-caliper design \u2014 same procedure as most American 4x4s of the era.","Z16 pad-and-rotor kit replaces both wear items at once for under $130.","Front brakes do 70% of stopping work; this is the highest-payoff brake maintenance you can do."],"cons":["1987\u20131989 vs 1990+ rotor/caliper sizes don't interchange; verify your year before ordering.","Caliper slide pins on neglected trucks corrode in their bores \u2014 plan for time and PB Blaster if your truck has sat outdoors.","Soft brake pedal after the job usually means air entered the line during piston compression; bleed if pedal doesn't firm up."]},{"id":"yj-budget-trail","title":"Budget Trail YJ \u2014 4-Inch Lift, 33s, and Lockers Done Right","vehicle":"yj","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"trail_build","type":"build_recipe","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":80},"cost_usd":{"low":2500,"high":6000},"tools_required":["full hand-tool set","floor jack + jack stands","torque wrench","spring compressor / leaf tools","angle finder","grinder"],"skills_required":["suspension + driveline work","planning a build in the right order"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["build_recipe","lift","33s","lockers","dana-35","trail-build"],"parts":[{"name":"Suspension lift (3-4 in spring or spring-over) + shocks","vendor":"Rough Country / OME / BDS","price_estimate_usd":700},{"name":"33x12.5R15 tires + wheels (set of 5)","vendor":"BFG/Cooper + steel wheels","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"Rear locker (selectable or lunchbox) for Dana 35/44","vendor":"Eaton / Yukon Grizzly / Aussie","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"SYE + CV driveshaft (lift companion)","vendor":"JB Conversions / Tom Woods","price_estimate_usd":550},{"name":"Regear 4.56 + install kits (front + rear)","vendor":"Yukon / Nitro","price_estimate_usd":700}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep YJ build planning \u2014 lift, tire, gear math references","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/"},{"title":"Spring-over vs spring lift for leaf-sprung YJ (Pirate4x4 tech)","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/"}],"alternatives":["yj-lift-kit-guide","yj-dana-35-rear-upgrade"],"summary":"A YJ is one of the best-value trail platforms you can build: short, light, solid-axle, and leaf-sprung, so it gets to 33s cheaply. The sane budget recipe is a 3-4 inch lift, 33x12.5R15 tires, a rear locker, a slip-yoke eliminator with a CV driveshaft, and a regear to 4.56 \u2014 in that order. Plan on $2,500-$6,000 depending on how much you do yourself and whether you regear. The mistake to avoid is bolting on big tires and a tall lift first and dealing with the driveline vibration, weak Dana 35, and lost gearing as afterthoughts.","description":"The YJ's leaf-sprung suspension and light weight are exactly what make it cheap to build. You do not need coilovers and long arms; you need the right springs, the driveline corrections a lift demands, and a plan that spends money in the order that actually improves the Jeep. Here is the build that works without wasting money.\n\nFor 33s, a **3-4 inch lift** clears them with minor trimming. You have two paths: a conventional **spring lift** (new taller leaf packs and longer shackles, simplest and most comfortable on-road) or a **spring-over axle (SOA)** conversion, which is cheap flex but raises the center of gravity and needs care with steering geometry and driveline angles. For most people a quality spring lift plus good shocks is the right call; SOA is for those chasing maximum articulation who will address the geometry. Run **33x12.5R15** on 15-inch wheels for the cheapest strong tire/wheel combo.\n\nA 4-inch YJ lift breaks two things you must fix: **driveline angle** and **gearing**. Add a **slip-yoke eliminator and CV rear driveshaft** to cure the vibration, and set the rear pinion angle to the new geometry. Then **regear to 4.56** so 33s feel like stock tires did \u2014 the YJ's factory 3.55/3.73 gears leave the engine gutless on 33s. Skipping the regear is the most common \"why is my built YJ so slow\" complaint.\n\nAdd a **rear locker** first \u2014 it is the single biggest capability upgrade for the money, and a selectable or lunchbox locker drops into the Dana 35 or 44. But know the **Dana 35 is the weak link**: it survives 33s with a locker if you drive smart, but hard use or 35s will break axle shafts. Budget for shaft upgrades or a Dana 44 swap if you push it. A front locker or limited-slip comes later, after the rear.\n\nDo it in this order: lift + shocks, tires/wheels, SYE + driveshaft, rear locker, regear, then armor and a front locker. Spending in this sequence means each step works with the last instead of needing redone. Get an alignment after the lift and steering work, and re-torque everything after a shakedown run.","pros":["The cheapest credible path to a real 33-inch trail rig","Leaf-sprung and light, so the YJ needs no exotic suspension","Build-order plan avoids the classic redo-it-twice money pit"],"cons":["The Dana 35 limits you \u2014 plan around it or upgrade it","A 4-inch lift mandates the SYE/driveshaft and regear, not optional","SOA is cheap flex but raises center of gravity \u2014 choose carefully"]},{"id":"yj-ax15-transmission","title":"AX-15 Manual Transmission Service \u2014 YJ (and the BA-10 Problem)","vehicle":"yj","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"transmission","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":1500},"tools_required":["transmission jack or helper","metric sockets","torque wrench","fluid pump","drain pan","snap-ring pliers (rebuild)"],"skills_required":["fluid service","clutch/transmission R&R for a swap or reseal"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1989-1995"],"tags":["drivetrain","transmission","ax-15","ba-10","manual","swap"],"parts":[{"name":"Manual transmission fluid (AX-15: 75W-90 GL-3 or Mopar spec)","vendor":"Pennzoil Synchromesh / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Shifter rebuild / bushing kit","vendor":"Crown / Novak","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Used/reman AX-15 (for a BA-10 swap)","vendor":"salvage / Advance Adapters","price_estimate_usd":600}],"citations":[{"title":"Novak Conversions \u2014 AX-15 transmission reference (Jeep)","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/ax15.htm"},{"title":"Jeep YJ factory service manual \u2014 manual transmission fluid spec","url":"https://www.jeep.com/"}],"alternatives":["yj-transfer-case-fluid","yj-sye-cv-driveshaft"],"summary":"The AX-15 is the good 5-speed behind the YJ 4.0L \u2014 a stout Aisin-built box that lives a long life on the right fluid and a working shifter. If you have an early (1987-1989) 4.2L or 4.0L YJ with the **Peugeot BA-10/5**, that transmission is the weak one, and the AX-15 is the standard, near-bolt-in upgrade. A fluid service is $40 and an hour; a BA-10-to-AX-15 swap is a weekend and a few hundred dollars in parts. Use the correct manual-trans fluid \u2014 the AX-15 hates the wrong oil and will notch into second when cold if you feed it the wrong stuff.","description":"Jeep used two very different transmissions behind the bigger YJ engines. The 1987-1989 trucks often got the **BA-10/5**, a Peugeot unit that is fragile and parts-scarce. From 1989 on, the **AX-15** (a Toyota/Aisin design) became the manual behind the 4.0L, and it is one of the better light-duty Jeep transmissions ever fitted. Knowing which you have decides whether you are doing maintenance or planning a swap.\n\nThe AX-15 is sensitive to fluid. The factory called for a specific GL-3 gear oil; many owners run a synchromesh-type manual transmission fluid (such as Pennzoil Synchromesh) for smoother cold shifts. Avoid heavy GL-5 hypoid gear oil \u2014 it can attack the synchros and the yellow-metal parts. Pull the fill plug first (if it will not come out, do not drain \u2014 you would be stuck), then the drain plug, and refill to the fill-hole level. A notchy second-gear shift when cold is the classic \"wrong fluid\" symptom and a fluid change often cures it.\n\nIf your YJ still has the BA-10/5, plan for it to fail and budget the AX-15 swap. The AX-15 bolts to the same 4.0L bellhousing pattern and mates to the NP207/NP231 transfer case, so the swap is mostly a matter of sourcing a good used AX-15, the matching clutch and bellhousing, and the correct shifter. It is the established, well-documented fix and turns a fragile driveline into a durable one. Source a unit from a 4.0L donor for the right input and gearing.\n\nA vague or rattly shifter is usually worn shifter bushings and the shift-tower components, not the transmission internals \u2014 rebuild the shifter before condemning the box. While the transmission is out for a reseal or swap, do the clutch, rear main seal, and pilot bearing as a set; you do not want to pull it twice. Refill, bleed the hydraulics if equipped, and verify clean engagement in all gears.","pros":["The AX-15 is durable and well-supported \u2014 a fluid service goes a long way","A BA-10-to-AX-15 swap permanently fixes the weak early driveline","Correct fluid cures the common cold-shift notchiness for cheap"],"cons":["Wrong fluid (GL-5 hypoid) damages synchros \u2014 read the spec","A full swap is a weekend job with clutch and sourcing involved","BA-10 parts are scarce; do not pour money into rebuilding one"]},{"id":"yj-sye-cv-driveshaft","title":"Slip-Yoke Eliminator and CV Driveshaft \u2014 YJ (NP231)","vehicle":"yj","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"transfer_case","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":800},"tools_required":["floor jack + jack stands","metric + SAE sockets","snap-ring pliers","torque wrench","pry bar","thread locker","drain pan"],"skills_required":["transfer-case disassembly (output/tailhousing)","driveline angle basics"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1988-1995"],"tags":["drivetrain","transfer-case","sye","driveshaft","vibration","np231","lift"],"parts":[{"name":"Slip-yoke eliminator kit (NP231, fixed yoke)","vendor":"JB Conversions / Advance Adapters","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Rear CV (double-cardan) driveshaft, YJ length","vendor":"Tom Woods / Adams Driveshaft","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Transfer-case fluid (ATF/MOPAR ATF+4 per build)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":25}],"citations":[{"title":"Tom Woods Driveshafts \u2014 SYE and CV driveshaft tech (Jeep NP231)","url":"https://www.4xshaft.com/"},{"title":"JB Conversions SYE installation instructions (NP231)","url":"https://www.jbconversions.com/"}],"alternatives":["yj-lift-kit-guide","yj-transfer-case-fluid"],"summary":"Once a YJ goes past about a 3-4 inch lift, the NP231's long factory slip-yoke driveshaft runs at too steep an angle and you get a vibration you cannot tune out. A slip-yoke eliminator (SYE) plus a double-cardan (CV) rear driveshaft fixes it at the source by shortening the tailhousing and letting the driveshaft operate at the correct compound angle. Budget $350-$800 for the kit and a custom-length shaft. This is the upgrade that makes a lifted YJ smooth again \u2014 skip it and you will chase driveline vibration forever.","description":"The NP231 transfer case used in 1988-1995 YJs has a slip-yoke output: the driveshaft slides in and out of a long tailhousing. At stock height it works fine, but lift the Jeep and the rear pinion angle changes, putting the single-joint slip-yoke shaft at a steep operating angle that vibrates and wears U-joints fast. The SYE replaces the slip output with a fixed yoke, and a CV driveshaft handles the steeper angle with a double-cardan joint at the transfer-case end.\n\nA standard driveshaft U-joint wants its operating angle matched at both ends, within a few degrees. Lift the YJ and you cannot satisfy that with the long factory slip-yoke shaft \u2014 you either get vibration or you rotate the pinion up so far that the slip-yoke binds. The SYE + CV combination breaks that compromise: the CV joint at the t-case end absorbs the angle, and you point the rear pinion straight at the CV joint instead of parallel to the driveshaft. It is the correct fix, not a band-aid.\n\nDrain the transfer case and support it. The SYE kit replaces the rear output shaft, bearing, and tailhousing \u2014 most kits are a \"short\" SYE that also shortens the case by a couple of inches (buying back driveshaft length). Follow the kit's order for pressing the speedometer/tone components and setting the snap ring. Torque the tailhousing bolts to spec, refill with the specified fluid, and check for leaks. Take careful measurements for the driveshaft after the SYE is in \u2014 the shaft is built to your exact pinion-to-yoke length.\n\nWith the SYE installed, set the rear axle pinion to point at the transfer-case CV joint (not parallel to the shaft) \u2014 a degree shim under the leaf springs or adjusting your lift's pinion correction gets you there. Measure center-to-center for the CV driveshaft and order it built to length. After install, test drive and check for vibration across the speed range; a remaining vibration usually means the pinion angle needs a small shim change.","pros":["Permanently cures lifted-YJ driveline vibration at the source","Fixed yoke is stronger and seals better than the slip output","Lets you run a proper pinion angle without slip-yoke bind"],"cons":["Requires opening the transfer case \u2014 more involved than a bolt-on","The CV driveshaft is a custom-length part, not off-the-shelf","Pinion angle must be set correctly or the vibration persists"]},{"id":"dual-battery-yj","title":"Dual Battery Setup for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"electrical","subcategory":"battery","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":650},"tools_required":["socket set","wire crimper","multimeter","drill","heat gun"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","wiring safety"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["electrical","battery","dual-battery","alternator","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"Optima Yellow Top D34/78 (auxiliary)","vendor":"Optima","price_estimate_usd":230},{"name":"Sure Power 1315A battery isolator","vendor":"Sure Power","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Battery Tender Plus charger","vendor":"Battery Tender","price_estimate_usd":50},{"name":"Auxiliary battery bracket/tray (YJ engine bay)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":60}],"citations":[{"title":"Optima Yellow Top D34/78 spec","url":"https://www.optimabatteries.com/d34-78"},{"title":"Sure Power battery isolator","url":"https://www.surepower.com/"}],"alternatives":["winch-wiring-yj","led-headlights-yj"],"summary":"The YJ's engine bay is smaller than a TJ or JK, but there is room for a secondary battery on a custom bracket mount. The 4.0L YJ's 90-amp alternator provides adequate charging headroom for a dual-battery setup running lights and a radio. The 4.2L YJ's smaller alternator limits how many accessories you can run simultaneously \u2014 calculate the draw budget before planning.","description":"A dual-battery setup on a YJ is most useful for: sustained winch use (having reserve capacity when the alternator can't keep up), running lights and a small compressor at camp, and cold-weather starts where the main battery is stressed by the starting demand.\n\nBefore buying batteries and isolators, calculate your accessory load. A winch at full pull draws 400+ amps \u2014 that's a burst load, not continuous. LED lights draw 5\u201315 amps. A tire inflator draws 20\u201330 amps. A fridge draws 3\u20135 amps continuously. On a 4.0L YJ with a 90-amp alternator, running LED lights (10A) and a compressor (25A) is manageable. Adding a fridge (5A) is fine. Adding a winch on a long pull exceeds the alternator's ability to keep up \u2014 that's where battery reserve matters.\n\nThe Optima Yellow Top D34/78 is the right auxiliary battery for a YJ. It's a deep-cycle AGM that tolerates the discharge-recharge cycles accessories demand. It's compact enough to mount in the YJ engine bay on a bracket. The standard Group 31 used in LJ dual setups is too large for most YJ engine bays.\n\nThe Sure Power 1315A battery isolator is adequate for a YJ used on trail and occasional camping. It keeps the batteries separated until alternator voltage rises, then charges both. The diode voltage drop means the batteries charge to slightly below alternator output voltage, which is acceptable for most use cases. For a dedicated overland YJ with a fridge and extended camp power needs, step up to a DC-DC charger.\n\nThe YJ that sits in the garage between trail runs benefits enormously from a maintenance charger. A Battery Tender Plus connected in storage prevents sulfation and ensures the batteries are at full capacity for the next trip. Essential for any YJ running AGM batteries."},{"id":"led-headlights-yj","title":"LED Headlight Upgrade for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"electrical","subcategory":"lighting","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":280},"tools_required":["flathead screwdriver","socket set"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["electrical","headlights","led","7-inch","lighting","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"JW Speaker 8700 Evolution J LED (7\", pair)","vendor":"JW Speaker","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Hella Vision Plus 7\" LED (pair)","vendor":"Hella","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Basic LED H4 7\" replacement (pair)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Load resistor kit (for hyper-flash prevention)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":20}],"citations":[{"title":"JW Speaker 8700 Evolution J spec","url":"https://www.jwspeaker.com/products/8700-evolution-j"},{"title":"7-inch LED headlight guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/"}],"alternatives":["winch-wiring-yj","dual-battery-yj"],"summary":"Let's clarify the YJ headlight geometry: the YJ has a distinctive square headlight bezel, but the actual headlight unit inside it is a 7-inch round H4 bulb. The \"square headlights\" that polarize Jeep fans are the bezels. The headlights themselves are a standard 7-inch round format that fits every aftermarket LED upgrade made for that size. Upgrading from factory sealed-beam halogens to LED is one of the easiest and most dramatic improvements on any YJ.","description":"Factory sealed-beam halogens on a YJ produce roughly 700\u2013900 lumens of yellow light. A modern 7-inch LED headlight produces 2,000\u20134,000 lumens of white light with a properly focused beam pattern. The difference is enormous on a dark dirt road and noticeable even in town.\n\nThe JW Speaker 8700 Evolution J is the premium 7-inch LED option. It's a DOT-compliant unit with a well-focused beam pattern that doesn't produce glare for oncoming traffic. The thermal management is excellent \u2014 these don't burn out from heat buildup the way cheap LED bulbs do. At $250/pair they're the most expensive option here but will outlast cheaper alternatives significantly. If you plan to daily the YJ or drive it long distances at night, the JW Speaker units are worth it.\n\nHella's Vision Plus 7-inch LED is a quality European-specification unit with a sharp beam cutoff. At $180/pair it's a strong value. The Hella units are especially good for road driving where beam pattern precision matters. For a YJ that drives real roads in addition to trails, Hella is the practical mid-tier pick.\n\nAt $40\u201360/pair, generic LED H4 replacements from Amazon and similar sources produce significantly more light than factory halogens and are a legitimate improvement. Build quality is inconsistent \u2014 check reviews for units with good thermal management and a proper beam focus. The risk is getting a unit that produces scattered light or has a short lifespan. For a trail-only YJ where nighttime driving is occasional, the budget option works.\n\nSome YJs equipped with electronic flasher modules will show a hyper-flash on the turn signals when LEDs are installed because LEDs draw less current than the flasher expects. A $20 load resistor kit per headlight circuit solves this. On older YJs with mechanical flashers, this is often not an issue \u2014 check after installation."},{"id":"winch-wiring-yj","title":"Winch Electrical Install for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"electrical","subcategory":"winch","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":800},"tools_required":["wire crimper","heat gun","multimeter","socket set","drill"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","wiring safety","terminal crimping"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["electrical","winch","wiring","battery","fuse","alternator","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn M8274-50 (8,274 lb, the classic YJ winch)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"Warn VR10000-S (10,000 lb, synthetic)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":800},{"name":"Superwinch Talon 9.5i (9,500 lb)","vendor":"Superwinch","price_estimate_usd":550},{"name":"2/0 AWG welding cable, 10-foot lengths (x2)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"150A ANL fuse + holder","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Alternator upgrade (4.2L YJ, 1987-1990)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn M8274-50 spec page","url":"https://www.warn.com/m8274-50"},{"title":"Warn VR10000-S","url":"https://www.warn.com/vr10000s"}],"alternatives":["dual-battery-yj","winch-yj","front-bumper-yj"],"summary":"The YJ's winch electrical situation splits by engine year. The 1991\u20131995 4.0L YJ has a 90-amp alternator that can support a mid-size winch with the same care as any 4.0L Wrangler. The 1987\u20131990 4.2L RENIX-era YJ has a 60\u201370 amp alternator that is not adequate for sustained winch use without an alternator upgrade first. Know which YJ you have before planning the winch install.","description":"This distinction matters because installing a 9,500-lb winch on a 4.2L YJ with a 60-amp alternator and then doing a hard recovery on a hill will discharge the battery faster than the alternator can recover. In the worst case, you lose electrical power mid-recovery in a remote location.\n\nBefore adding a winch to a 1987\u20131990 4.2L YJ, upgrade the alternator to a higher-output unit. A 90-amp or 105-amp alternator swap is possible using compatible units from the 4.0L era. This is a moderate difficulty swap that significantly improves the electrical headroom for a winch. Budget $150\u2013200 for a quality remanufactured 90A unit.\n\nThe 4.0L YJ's 90-amp alternator supports a 9,500\u201312,000 lb winch with the same operational caveats as a TJ or LJ: run the engine at 1,500 RPM during sustained winching to maximize alternator output, and avoid prolonged pull sequences that drain the battery below 12 volts.\n\nThe Warn M8274-50 is the legendary YJ winch choice \u2014 an above-bumper mount electric winch with a long history of trail use. It's heavier than a bumper-mount winch and requires a specific bumper configuration, but it's mechanically excellent and rebuildable. A classic choice for a dedicated trail YJ.\n\nFor a bumper-mount setup, the Warn VR10000-S and Superwinch Talon 9.5i are the practical modern options. The VR10000-S is sealed, reliable, and lighter than the M8274. The Talon 9.5i is the budget-value pick.\n\nSame rule as every winch install: 2/0 AWG cable direct from the battery, 150A ANL fuse within 18 inches of the positive terminal, proper ring terminals with heat shrink. Do not tap factory circuits. The YJ's factory wiring is not sized for winch loads."},{"id":"yj-258-to-40-swap","title":"4.2L 258 to 4.0L HO Swap \u2014 What It Takes (YJ)","vehicle":"yj","category":"engine","subcategory":"engine_swap","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":60},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":4000},"tools_required":["engine hoist + stand","full metric + SAE socket sets","torque wrench","multimeter","engine leveler","transmission jack"],"skills_required":["full engine R&R","wiring harness integration","driveline and accessory fitment"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990"],"tags":["engine","swap","4.0l","258","ho","fuel-injection"],"parts":[{"name":"Donor 4.0L HO engine + harness + PCM (1991-1995 YJ/XJ)","vendor":"salvage","price_estimate_usd":1200},{"name":"Motor mounts / adapter for 4.0L in YJ","vendor":"Crown / Novak","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Fuel system: return-style pump, lines, regulator","vendor":"Mopar / aftermarket","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Radiator, exhaust, and accessory brackets to suit 4.0L","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":400}],"citations":[{"title":"Novak Conversions \u2014 AMC 4.0L into Jeep swap reference","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/40.htm"},{"title":"NAXJA / Jeep forums \u2014 258 to 4.0L HO swap writeups","url":"https://www.naxja.org/"}],"alternatives":["yj-hard-start-no-start","yj-ax15-transmission"],"summary":"Swapping the carbureted 4.2L 258 for a fuel-injected 4.0L HO is the single biggest drivability upgrade for an early YJ: more power, far better cold starts, modern fuel injection, and a stronger AX-15 behind it if you swap the trans too. It is a real project \u2014 20 to 60 hours and $800-$4,000 depending on how complete your donor is \u2014 but the parts share a family lineage, so it bolts in more cleanly than most engine swaps. The make-or-break detail is the wiring: do the harness and PCM integration right and the rest is mechanical.","description":"The AMC 258 (4.2L) is a torquey, durable engine, but in carbureted YJ form it is down on power and finicky in the cold and at altitude. The 4.0L HO that replaced it (1991+) makes about 180 hp with sequential fuel injection and starts instantly in any weather. Because both are AMC inline-sixes sharing the same basic block architecture and bellhousing pattern, the 4.0L drops into the YJ engine bay with known mounts and mates to Jeep transmissions.\n\nThe cleanest path is a complete 1991-1995 4.0L HO donor from a YJ or XJ: engine, the full engine wiring harness, the PCM, and the fuel-injection components. Buying these as a matched set from one vehicle saves enormous headaches versus assembling piecemeal. A YJ donor is ideal because the accessory layout and motor mounts match your chassis most closely. Inspect the donor for the renowned 0331 head-crack issue before you buy.\n\nThe 258 is carbureted, so your YJ has no fuel-injection harness or computer. You will integrate the 4.0L engine harness and PCM, add a return-style fuel system (an injected engine needs supply and return plus the right pressure), and sort out the ignition, sensors, and gauges. This is where swaps succeed or fail \u2014 take your time labeling and routing the harness, and use a known-good donor PCM matched to the engine and transmission type.\n\nPlan for a 4.0L-spec radiator (the injected engine and its electric fan setup differ from the carb truck), the correct exhaust manifold and downpipe, and accessory brackets that match the 4.0L. If you are also moving to the AX-15 (recommended over a BA-10), do the clutch, bellhousing, and crossmember in the same project. Set motor mounts and pinion/driveline angles, then commission the fuel and ignition systems carefully on first start.","pros":["Huge gain in power, cold-start, and all-around drivability","Family-shared architecture makes it a relatively clean swap","Pairs naturally with the stronger AX-15 transmission upgrade"],"cons":["Big time investment and a real budget, especially if the donor is incomplete","Wiring/PCM integration is the hard part \u2014 not a weekend job","Needs fuel-system, cooling, and exhaust changes, not only the engine"]},{"id":"yj-hard-start-no-start","title":"Hard-Start / No-Start Diagnosis \u2014 YJ 4.0L, 4.2L, and RENIX","vehicle":"yj","category":"engine","subcategory":"diagnostics","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":250},"tools_required":["multimeter","noid light or injector test light","fuel pressure gauge","spark tester","basic hand tools"],"skills_required":["systematic spark/fuel/air diagnosis","reading a wiring diagram"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["engine","no-start","hard-start","crank-position-sensor","renix","diagnostics"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"International Full Size Jeep / 4.0L tech \u2014 crank position sensor no-start","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/"},{"title":"RENIX vs HO 4.0L diagnosis reference (NAXJA tech)","url":"https://www.naxja.org/"}],"alternatives":["yj-spark-plugs","yj-258-to-40-swap"],"summary":"A YJ that cranks but will not start is almost always a spark, fuel, or sensor problem with a short list of usual suspects \u2014 and on the fuel-injected 4.0L, the **crankshaft position sensor (CPS)** is the number-one cause of a no-start or intermittent hot-start. Diagnose in order: confirm spark, confirm fuel pressure, then check the CPS and grounds before buying anything. The work is mostly free with a multimeter; the fix is usually a $40-$120 sensor. Resist the parts-cannon \u2014 a YJ no-start rewards a methodical spark-fuel-air check.","description":"YJs came with three very different fuel/ignition setups: the carbureted **4.2L 258**, the **RENIX** fuel-injected 4.0L (1987-1990), and the **HO** 4.0L (1991-1995) with the more familiar Chrysler injection. The diagnostic order is the same for all three \u2014 spark, then fuel, then the sensor and grounds \u2014 but where you look differs by system.\n\nPull a plug wire, fit a spark tester, and crank. **No spark** on the injected 4.0L points hard at the **crankshaft position sensor** (mounted at the bellhousing, reading the flywheel) \u2014 a failing CPS is the classic \"ran fine, now cranks and won't start, especially when hot\" complaint. Check the CPS connector and resistance against spec before condemning it. On the carbureted 258, no spark sends you to the ignition module, coil, and the pickup in the distributor. Weak or intermittent spark on any of them often traces to corroded grounds \u2014 the YJ's engine and body grounds are a common root cause.\n\nOn the injected engines, key on and listen for the in-tank pump prime; put a gauge on the fuel rail and verify pressure to spec (and that it holds). No prime points at the pump, relay, or ASD circuit. A noid light at the injector connector confirms the injectors are being pulsed. On the carbureted 258, check that the mechanical pump delivers fuel and that the carburetor is not flooded or dry.\n\nIf spark and fuel both check out but it still will not start, suspect the CPS (injected) or the ignition pickup (carb), and clean every engine-to-body and battery ground \u2014 RENIX 4.0L systems in particular are famously sensitive to ground quality and will throw odd no-start and stalling behavior when grounds are corroded. Refresh the grounds at the head, the firewall, and the battery before chasing ghosts. Only after spark, fuel, and grounds are verified should you look at the ECU or wiring harness.","pros":["A clear spark-then-fuel-then-sensor order solves most YJ no-starts fast","The likely fix (CPS or a ground refresh) is cheap","Most of the diagnosis costs nothing but a multimeter and time"],"cons":["Three different systems (carb / RENIX / HO) need slightly different checks","A hot-only no-start (CPS) can fool you by restarting once cooled","Corroded grounds mimic sensor and ignition failures \u2014 check them early","This is a diagnostic guide; on a YJ you may also be chasing it on the trail, so carry a spare CPS once you have confirmed it as your weak point."]},{"id":"seats-yj","title":"Seat Upgrade for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"interior","subcategory":"seats","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":1000},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["interior","seats","comfort","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"Corbeau LG1 High Back Seat (each)","vendor":"Corbeau","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Foam Seat (each)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"Mastercraft Baja RS Reclining Seat (each)","vendor":"Mastercraft","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Universal seat bracket adapter kit","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Corbeau LG1 seat specs","url":"https://www.corbeau.com/lg1"},{"title":"Smittybilt XRC seat","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/smittybilt/xrc-seat"}],"alternatives":["tire-sizing-yj"],"summary":"The YJ's factory seats are a common complaint among owners \u2014 vinyl-covered foam over a minimal frame, with no lateral support for trail use and no real comfort on a highway drive. The good news is that aftermarket seat options are plentiful and installation is straightforward: the YJ's floor mounting points accept standard bolt patterns with a universal bracket adapter.","description":"Unlike the TJ and LJ where many seats are specifically designed as direct-fit, YJ seats typically require a universal bracket adapter to match the seat's mounting pattern to the YJ's floor holes. This adds about $45 and 15 minutes to the installation but is otherwise not a limitation.\n\nThe Corbeau LG1 is the go-to seat upgrade for a YJ used as a daily driver or overlander. It provides genuine lateral bolstering, a tall backrest for highway driving support, and a reclining mechanism that makes it livable for longer drives. The fiberglass shell holds shape under trail use. At $280 per seat it's the right price for the quality improvement. Install with a universal Corbeau or aftermarket bracket.\n\nAt $150/seat the Smittybilt XRC is a meaningful improvement over factory. The foam is firmer and more supportive; the high back helps on rough terrain. It's not a premium seat but it's a real upgrade. For a budget YJ build where money is better spent on drivetrain and armor, the XRC delivers results without breaking the bank.\n\nThe Mastercraft Baja RS is the serious performance seat option. Significant lateral bolstering, quality materials, and a design intended for rally and rock crawler use where the driver needs to stay planted. At $480/seat it's a commitment \u2014 appropriate for a dedicated trail YJ where the driver spends hours at a time in demanding terrain.\n\nWhen installing aftermarket seats in a YJ, inspect the floor mounting points for rust before bolting in the new seats. YJ floors are notorious for rust at the seat mounting locations, particularly on vehicles from wet climates. If the mounting plate or floor is compromised, address it before installing the seats. A seat that isn't properly anchored to the floor is a safety hazard."},{"id":"yj-serpentine-belt","title":"Serpentine Belt Replacement","vehicle":"yj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":60},"tools_required":["1/2\" breaker bar or ratchet","15mm wrench","13mm wrench","belt-routing diagram"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["engine","drive-belt","amc-258","amc-4.0"],"parts":[{"name":"Goodyear Gatorback Poly-V belt (4.0L w/ AC)","part_number":"4060882","vendor":"Goodyear/Continental","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Gates Micro-V belt (4.0L w/ AC)","part_number":"K060882","vendor":"Gates","price_estimate_usd":26},{"name":"Mopar belt tensioner (later YJ w/ tensioner)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"4.0L serpentine belt routing \u2014 getahelmet.com","url":"https://www.getahelmet.com/jeeps/maint/belt/"},{"title":"YJ 2.5L/4.0L tensioner bolt \u2014 eBay listing","url":"https://www.ebay.com/itm/235878771846"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The YJ 4.0L runs a single serpentine belt around the crank, water pump, alternator, power steering, AC, and (on some years) a tensioner pulley. Plan on 30 minutes for the swap, longer if it's your first time figuring out the routing on an AC-equipped truck.","description":"The 1991\u20131995 YJ 4.0L uses a Poly-V serpentine belt that drives every accessory off the crankshaft. The 4.2L 258 in 1987\u20131990 uses an older multi-belt setup with V-belts for AC, alternator, and power steering \u2014 this guide covers both, but the serpentine belt swap on the 4.0L is the more common job. **Goodyear Gatorback 4060882** (or the Gates equivalent **K060882**) is the right belt for AC-equipped 4.0L trucks. Non-AC trucks run a shorter belt; check the parts catalog by your specific configuration before ordering.\n\nBelt tensioning on the YJ varies by year and accessory layout. Some 4.0L YJs run a spring-loaded automatic tensioner; others use a manual adjustment where you loosen the power steering pump bracket and pry the pump outward against a slot. **Take a photo of the existing belt routing before you remove anything** \u2014 the routing diagram under the hood is often faded or missing on a 30-year-old Jeep, and routing the new belt incorrectly will overheat the engine within minutes if the water pump spins backward.\n\nInspect the pulleys with the belt off. The water pump pulley should spin smoothly with no roughness or play. Power steering and alternator bearings should feel the same. A whining or growling bearing means you're going back in within a few months \u2014 replace it now while the belt is off. Check the tensioner pulley if equipped; a dry bearing or sloppy arm will eat belts.\n\nInstall the new belt by routing it around every pulley except the smoothest accessory (usually power steering or the tensioner pulley) last. Apply tension, double-check that every groove is fully seated in every pulley, and start the engine for 30 seconds. The belt should track straight without squealing. A new belt sometimes chirps for the first few minutes \u2014 if it persists, your routing is wrong or a pulley is misaligned.","steps":["Photograph the current belt routing; download the FSM diagram for backup.","Locate the tensioner (automatic) or adjustment bolt (manual on power steering).","Rotate the tensioner with a 1/2-inch breaker bar to slacken the belt; on manual setups, loosen the pump bracket and pry inward.","Slip the belt off the smoothest pulley first; remove from each accessory.","Spin every pulley by hand \u2014 replace anything rough or noisy now.","Route the new belt to match your photo; seat fully in every groove.","Reapply tension; on auto-tensioner, let it self-tension; on manual, set deflection per FSM (about 1/2-inch at the longest run).","Start the engine for 30 seconds; verify the belt tracks straight and pulleys spin true.","Reinspect tension after 100 miles of driving \u2014 new belts stretch."],"pros":["One belt drives every accessory on the 4.0L \u2014 far more straightforward than the 258's multi-belt setup.","Aftermarket Gates and Goodyear belts cost $25\u2013$30 and last 60,000 miles.","Catches dying bearings before they strand you."],"cons":["AC-equipped trucks have tight clearance around the compressor \u2014 knuckle skin is at risk.","Routing diagrams under the hood are often faded; photograph before disassembly.","Mismatched belt length (AC vs non-AC) is the most common ordering mistake."]},{"id":"yj-brake-fluid-flush","title":"Brake Fluid Flush","vehicle":"yj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":30},"tools_required":["10mm box-end wrench","clear vinyl tubing","catch bottle","turkey baster","fresh DOT 3 brake fluid (2 qt)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","patience for two-person work"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["brakes","fluids","safety","dot-3"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline DOT 3 brake fluid, 32 oz","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Prestone DOT 3 brake fluid, 32 oz","vendor":"Prestone","price_estimate_usd":7},{"name":"Speed bleeder check-valve bleeders (set of 4)","vendor":"Speed Bleeder","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"AutoZone \u2014 YJ Bleeding Brake System","url":"https://www.autozone.com/repairguides/Jeep-Wrangler-YJ-1987-1995-Repair-Guide/BRAKE-OPERATING-SYSTEM/Bleeding-Brake-System/_/P-0900c1528008afcb"},{"title":"Brake fluid flush procedure \u2014 Wrangler TJ Forum (same architecture)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/questions-about-flushing-brake-fluid.18804/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The YJ uses DOT 3 brake fluid. Flush every two years or 30,000 miles in the order furthest-from-master-cylinder first: passenger rear \u2192 driver rear \u2192 passenger front \u2192 driver front. The whole job takes about an hour with a helper.","description":"Brake fluid is hygroscopic \u2014 it absorbs water from the atmosphere through the seals, master cylinder cap, and flex hoses. Water-saturated fluid boils at a lower temperature, which means pedal fade during long descents, and it corrodes the inside of every steel brake line, caliper bore, and wheel cylinder. The YJ's brake system is unsophisticated (no ABS, no proportioning valve drama) but every component is steel or cast iron, so neglected fluid eats the system from the inside out. Flush every two years and the master cylinder, calipers, and wheel cylinders will outlive the truck.\n\nDOT 3 is the factory spec. DOT 4 is acceptable as an upgrade \u2014 it has a higher dry boiling point and the same chemistry, so it mixes cleanly with existing DOT 3. Do not use DOT 5 silicone fluid; it is not compatible with DOT 3/4 systems and will destroy the seals. Stick with DOT 3 from any major brand (Valvoline, Prestone, Wagner) and you're fine. Two 32-ounce bottles is enough for a full flush with margin.\n\nThe bleeding sequence on a YJ is the same as any non-ABS truck: **start at the wheel furthest from the master cylinder and work back**. That's passenger-side rear first, then driver-side rear, then passenger-side front, then driver-side front. The rear wheels use wheel cylinders with the bleeder on top; the fronts use caliper bleeders also on top. Every bleeder is 10mm. Crack each one before you raise the truck \u2014 frozen bleeders are common, and breaking one off means a wheel cylinder or caliper replacement.\n\nThe two-person method is: helper pumps the pedal three times slowly and holds; you open the bleeder, watch fluid jet out, close before the pedal hits the floor; helper releases. Repeat until clean fluid runs and no air bubbles come out. **Keep the master cylinder reservoir at least three-quarters full the entire time** \u2014 let it run dry and you'll pull air into the master, doubling the job. Suction the old fluid out of the reservoir with a turkey baster before you start to speed the flush.","steps":["Crack all four bleeder screws before raising the truck; replace any that won't break loose.","Suction old fluid out of the master cylinder reservoir with a turkey baster.","Refill master cylinder with fresh DOT 3 to the upper mark.","Attach clear tubing to passenger-rear bleeder; submerge other end in catch bottle.","Helper pumps brake pedal three times slowly, holds firm; open bleeder, watch fluid, close before pedal bottoms; helper releases.","Repeat at passenger-rear until clean fluid runs clear with no bubbles (3\u20135 cycles typical).","Top off master cylinder; move to driver-rear, then passenger-front, then driver-front, repeating step 5\u20136.","Final check: pedal should be firm; test in driveway before road test.","Recheck fluid level after first 20 miles; top up if needed."],"pros":["$15 of fluid prevents $300 of corroded caliper rebuilds down the road.","DOT 4 is a no-downside upgrade for towing or desert driving.","Doing this every two years means the master cylinder, calipers, and wheel cylinders likely outlive the truck."],"cons":["Old bleeders strip or snap on neglected trucks \u2014 budget for replacements before tearing into a 30-year-old system.","Letting the master cylinder run dry doubles the job; stay disciplined with refills.","DOT 5 silicone fluid is a one-way trip \u2014 never put it in a system that's seen DOT 3."]},{"id":"yj-coolant-flush","title":"Coolant Flush \u2014 4.0L and 258","vehicle":"yj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"cooling","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":75},"tools_required":["drain pan","garden hose","channel-lock pliers","torque wrench","funnel"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","patience for bleeding air"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["cooling","engine","thermostat","amc-258","amc-4.0","overheating"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar 195\u00b0F thermostat","part_number":"83501426","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Stant 195\u00b0F thermostat","part_number":"13456","vendor":"Stant","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Thermostat housing gasket","vendor":"Felpro","price_estimate_usd":6},{"name":"Prestone 50/50 antifreeze, 1 gal","vendor":"Prestone","price_estimate_usd":16}],"citations":[{"title":"Mishimoto YJ/TJ racing thermostat application","url":"https://www.mishimoto.com/jeep-wrangler-2-5l-4-0l-4-2l-racing-thermostat-87-06.html"},{"title":"Quadratec 1987-1995 YJ cooling system","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/vehicle/1987-1995-wrangler-yj/engine-and-fuel-system/cooling-system"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Open the petcock at the bottom of the radiator, drain into a pan, refill with a 50/50 mix of distilled water and full-strength antifreeze, run with the heater on max to bleed air, and top off \u2014 total system capacity is about 10 quarts.","description":"YJ cooling systems are old enough that the original coolant has been in the truck longer than most readers have owned the truck. Antifreeze breaks down chemically over time \u2014 the corrosion inhibitors deplete first, then the dielectric properties \u2014 and used coolant becomes mildly acidic, which eats heater cores, head gaskets, and water pumps from the inside. A coolant flush every three years or 36,000 miles is the cheapest preventative you can do.\n\nThe system holds **10 quarts (2.5 gallons)** on a 4.0L and slightly less on a 4.2L 258 \u2014 figure two gallons of pre-mixed 50/50 plus an extra gallon for top-off. The factory specified the green ethylene glycol \u2014 Prestone, Peak, or Zerex are all fine. Avoid mixing colors (orange Dex-Cool, gold, etc.) because the chemistries fight each other and form a gel. Always use distilled water if you mix your own \u2014 tap water deposits minerals on the inside of the head and radiator over time.\n\nThe thermostat is a 195\u00b0F unit and lives under the housing at the front of the head. While you have the system drained, swap it. Replace the housing gasket at the same time \u2014 they cost $6 and are the most common leak point after a thermostat job. Torque the housing bolts to **17 ft-lbs**; over-torquing cracks the cast housing. If the original housing is corroded or has cracks around the bolt holes, replace it now with a Mopar or Crown aluminum housing. Plastic aftermarket housings warp under heat and are not worth the savings.\n\nBleeding air is the step most people get wrong. Park the truck nose-up on a hill or ramps so the radiator cap is the highest point in the system. Refill with the cap off, start the engine, set the heater to maximum heat with the fan on low, and let it warm up until the upper radiator hose is hot. The thermostat opens, the air burps out, the coolant level drops \u2014 top it off. Squeeze the upper hose a few times to help. Cap on, drive around the block, let it cool overnight, recheck level in the overflow bottle in the morning.","steps":["Cold engine. Place 3+ gallon drain pan under radiator petcock (driver-side bottom of radiator).","Open petcock with channel-locks; let radiator drain.","Drain block: remove the block drain plug (passenger side of block, 1/2\" pipe plug) \u2014 catches another 2\u20133 quarts.","Close drains. Optional: fill with water + flush product, run to temp, drain again.","Remove thermostat housing; clean mating surface; install new thermostat (spring side toward engine) with new gasket.","Torque housing bolts to 17 ft-lbs.","Park nose-up on ramps. Fill radiator and overflow bottle with 50/50 distilled water + antifreeze.","Start engine, heat on max, fan on low, cap off.","Run until upper hose is hot and thermostat opens. Top off radiator as level drops.","Cap on, drive around the block, cool overnight, recheck overflow level in morning."],"pros":["Renews corrosion protection \u2014 single biggest cause of YJ head gasket failures is depleted coolant.","Forced opportunity to inspect hoses, clamps, and water pump weep hole.","Combines well with a thermostat replacement; both share the same drain step."],"cons":["Air bleeding is fiddly; expect to top off three or four times over the first few drives.","Heater core can air-lock and stay cold even after radiator is full \u2014 squeeze upper hose and rev to clear.","Used coolant is toxic to pets and wildlife \u2014 keep the drain pan covered and dispose at a recycling center."]},{"id":"yj-differential-fluid","title":"Differential Fluid Change \u2014 Dana 30, Dana 35, Dana 44","vehicle":"yj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain_fluids","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":70},"tools_required":["3/8 drive ratchet (Dana 30 fill plug)","flathead screwdriver (Dana 35 fill plug)","13mm or 1/2 wrench for cover bolts","RTV silicone or cover gasket","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","cover-gasket discipline"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["fluids","axles","dana-30","dana-35","dana-44"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline 80W-90 gear oil, 1 qt","part_number":"773723","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Mobil 1 75W-90 synthetic gear oil, 1 qt","part_number":"104361","vendor":"Mobil","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Lucas limited-slip additive (Trac-Lok rears only)","vendor":"Lucas","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Permatex Ultra Black RTV","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Mopar Dana 35 cover gasket","part_number":"5083660AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Forum \u2014 Dana 30 and Dana 35 fluid change capacities","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/dana-30-and-dana-35-differential-fluid-change-help.41836/"},{"title":"Motobilt \u2014 Wrangler differential fluid capacity chart","url":"https://motobilt.com/blogs/first-blog/jeep-wrangler-differential-fluid-capacity-chart"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Pull the cover, drain, scrape the old RTV clean, lay a 1/8\" bead of new RTV around the cover flange, torque cover bolts in star pattern, and refill through the fill plug until oil dribbles out \u2014 Dana 30 takes about 1 quart, Dana 35 takes 2 quarts, Dana 44 takes about 3 quarts.","description":"Differential fluid is one of those services that's invisible until it kills the axle. The oil sees high heat, fine metal particles from normal gear wear, and water intrusion if you ford streams or wash with a pressure washer. Change interval is 30,000 miles for street driving, 15,000 miles or annually for trail use, and immediately after any water crossing where you suspect the axle vent ingested water (cloudy or milky oil = water contamination \u2014 change it now or lose the carrier bearings).\n\nYJ axle math: the front is a **Dana 30** on every YJ, about 1 quart capacity. The rear is a **Dana 35** on 4-cylinder and most 6-cylinder YJs (~2 quarts), or an optional **Dana 44** on a small number of trucks (~3 quarts). If your rear cover has 10 bolts and a flat-bottom shape, it's a Dana 35. If it has 10 bolts but a more rounded shape and is noticeably larger, it's a Dana 44. Check the tag on the cover or the casting if you're unsure.\n\nThe Dana 30 has a square-headed fill plug at the front-passenger side of the housing \u2014 a 3/8\" drive ratchet fits directly into the square hole. The Dana 35 fill plug is a rubber-and-metal stopper that you pry off with a flathead screwdriver. Most YJs don't have a drain plug \u2014 you pull the cover to drain. Plan for that: have a new gasket or a tube of RTV, a wire brush to scrape the old sealant, and brake cleaner to degrease the mating surface. Cover bolt torque is **20 ft-lbs** for Dana 30 and Dana 35, **30 ft-lbs** for Dana 44. Tighten in a star pattern in three passes \u2014 even pressure prevents cover-flange warp.\n\nFluid choice: 80W-90 conventional gear oil is the factory spec. 75W-90 synthetic shifts a bit more manageable in cold weather and runs slightly cooler under load \u2014 worth the extra $6 per quart on trail-driven rigs. If your rear has the Trac-Lok limited-slip clutch pack (most YJs do not \u2014 it was optional), add 4 oz of friction modifier or use a gear oil rated for limited-slip use, or the clutches will chatter on tight turns.\n\nFill until oil dribbles out the fill hole with the axle level \u2014 that's the correct level by design. Over-filling pushes oil past seals and onto your brake drums. Underfilling starves the ring gear and burns the pinion bearing. Both axles have a vent tube on top \u2014 pull the vent hose and verify it isn't crushed or plugged before you drive away.","steps":["Park on level ground. Loosen fill plug FIRST (verify you can get it out \u2014 if it's seized, don't drain the axle).","Position drain pan under differential cover.","Remove all-but-two cover bolts on the upper half of the cover; loosen those two.","Pry cover loose at the bottom with a flathead \u2014 oil drains. Catch in pan.","Remove last two bolts; remove cover.","Wipe inside of housing clean. Inspect ring gear and check magnet at bottom of cover (fine fuzz = normal; chunks = stop and rebuild).","Scrape old RTV from cover and housing flanges; degrease with brake cleaner.","Apply 1/8\" bead of RTV around cover flange OR install new gasket.","Reinstall cover; torque bolts in star pattern: 20 ft-lbs (D30/D35) or 30 ft-lbs (D44), three passes.","Let RTV cure 1 hour before adding fluid.","Pump fresh gear oil through fill plug until oil dribbles out level.","Reinstall fill plug; torque to 25 ft-lbs (D30/D35) or 35 ft-lbs (D44).","Verify axle vent tube is clear; reattach if disconnected.","Repeat for other axle."],"pros":["Smallest investment in the longest-life drivetrain component on the truck.","Inspecting the cover magnet on every change catches gear failure months before catastrophic noise.","Synthetic 75W-90 lasts 50% longer than conventional under trail abuse."],"cons":["No drain plug on the cover means a messy job \u2014 paper towels and degreaser everywhere.","Dana 35 is the platform's weakest link; if you find chunks on the magnet, plan an axle upgrade (Dana 44 or Ford 8.8 swap).","RTV cure time of an hour is non-negotiable; rushing fill leaks the cover."]},{"id":"yj-oil-change","title":"Oil Change \u2014 4.0L and AMC 258","vehicle":"yj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"fluids","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":60},"tools_required":["13mm socket","oil filter wrench","drain pan (6+ qt)","funnel","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["fluids","engine","amc-258","amc-4.0"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar oil filter (4.0L, 1991-1995)","part_number":"5281090AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Mopar oil filter (4.2L 258, 1987-1990)","part_number":"33004195","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Mobil 1 5W-30 full synthetic, 5 qt","vendor":"Mobil","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"Valvoline conventional 10W-30, 5 qt","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":22}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar 4.0L oil filter 5281090AB","url":"https://www.jeepworld.com/products/4-0l-oil-filter-5281090ab"},{"title":"Wrangler YJ oil filter listing","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/omix-ada-oil-filter-yj-1743607.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Drop the 13mm drain plug, spin off the filter on the passenger side, refill with 6 quarts of 10W-30 (4.0L) or 5W-30 (4.0L in cold climates), and tighten everything to spec \u2014 first oil change on a YJ takes about 30 minutes once you've done it.","description":"The AMC inline-six in the YJ runs forever on clean oil and falls apart on neglected oil. Both engines \u2014 the 4.2L 258 (1987\u20131990) and the 4.0L HO (1991\u20131995) \u2014 share an oil-pan layout, a side-mounted spin-on filter, and a forgiveness for cheap conventional oil if you change it often. The 4.0L holds 6 quarts with filter; the 4.2L 258 holds 5 quarts with filter. Use 10W-30 for desert and summer, 5W-30 if you see freezing temps. Full synthetic is a real upgrade after 100k miles when the rings start letting a little blow-by through.\n\nMopar's filter for the 1991\u20131995 4.0L is **5281090AB** (M0-090 on some packaging). The 4.2L 258 takes the older **33004195**. Wix, Bosch, and Mobil 1 all make a fitting alternative \u2014 anything with the right thread and gasket size will work, but the Mopar filter has the strongest anti-drainback valve, which matters on a side-mounted filter where oil can siphon back into the pan on shutdown.\n\nWarm the engine for five minutes before draining \u2014 warm oil carries more suspended particles out with it. The drain plug on the pan is a 13mm hex head; the torque spec is **22 ft-lbs**. The filter sits horizontal on the passenger side of the block above the starter \u2014 a swivel-handle filter wrench or the cap-style wrench made for this filter size makes it manageable from the side. Spin the new filter on by hand until the gasket touches the block, then another three-quarters turn. Don't gorilla it \u2014 the gasket does the sealing, not the torque.\n\nAfter refill, run the engine for 30 seconds, shut it down, wait two minutes, and recheck the dipstick. Top up to the upper mark. Drive it around the block and check for drips at the filter and drain plug. Log the mileage. The AMC six wants fresh oil every 3,000 miles on conventional or 5,000 miles on synthetic \u2014 the engine will thank you with another 100,000 miles of service.","steps":["Warm engine 5 minutes, then park level on jack stands or ramps.","Position drain pan under the oil pan plug (driver side, rear of pan).","Remove 13mm drain plug; let drain 10\u201315 minutes until it stops.","Move drain pan under filter (passenger side, above starter); spin filter off counter-clockwise.","Wipe filter mounting surface clean; check old gasket came off with the filter.","Lubricate new filter gasket with fresh oil; hand-tighten until gasket contacts block + 3/4 turn.","Reinstall drain plug; torque to 22 ft-lbs (use a fresh crush washer if your plug uses one).","Refill: 6 qt for 4.0L, 5 qt for 4.2L 258 with filter; start engine for 30 sec; verify no leaks.","Shut off, wait 2 min, recheck dipstick, top to upper mark."],"pros":["Side-mounted filter is reachable without ramps if you have long arms.","Both AMC sixes tolerate any major-brand 5W-30 or 10W-30.","5,000-mile synthetic intervals work fine on engines under 150k miles."],"cons":["Side filter drips oil down the starter and onto the crossmember every time \u2014 keep paper towels nearby.","Mopar 5281090AB is harder to find than aftermarket equivalents; order ahead.","Crush washer on drain plug should be replaced \u2014 many people skip and end up with weeping plugs."]},{"id":"yj-spark-plugs","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 4.0L and 258","vehicle":"yj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"ignition","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":40},"tools_required":["5/8 spark plug socket","3-inch extension","torque wrench","anti-seize","gap tool"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["ignition","engine","amc-258","amc-4.0","tune-up"],"parts":[{"name":"Champion RC12LYC (set of 6, copper)","part_number":"RC12LYC","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Champion RC12YC (set of 6, copper)","part_number":"RC12YC","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"NGK ZFR5N (set of 6)","part_number":"ZFR5N","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"RC12LYC application \u2014 87-98 2.5/4.0L YJ and TJ","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/jeep-wrangler-rc12lyc-spark-plug-56027275.html"},{"title":"Spark plug torque 4.0L discussion","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/spark-plug-torque.227809/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Pull the wires one at a time, swap each Champion RC12LYC (gapped to 0.035\") with a dab of anti-seize on the threads, torque to 27 ft-lbs, and call it done in under an hour.","description":"The AMC inline-six is one of the friendliest engines on the planet for a spark plug change. All six plugs sit in a straight line along the passenger side of the head, each one exposed and reachable without removing a thing. The whole job is 30 to 45 minutes the first time, and 20 minutes after that. Plug life on copper Champions is 30,000 miles; platinum aftermarket equivalents can stretch to 60,000 miles but the engine doesn't run any better on them \u2014 the HEI-spec ignition on the 4.0L makes great spark either way.\n\nThe factory plug is the Champion **RC12LYC** (or **RC12YC** on early YJs) \u2014 both interchange. Gap is **0.035\"** on engines with the distributor cap (which covers every YJ); set the gap with a feeler gauge or a wire gap tool before installing. NGK and Autolite make crossover plugs that work fine \u2014 what matters is the heat range, which is what the 12 designates. Don't run plugs cooler than the OE heat range unless your engine is built; you'll foul them at idle.\n\nPull one wire at a time and replace one plug at a time \u2014 that way you cannot mix up the firing order. The boots get stiff with age; twist before pulling. Use a 5/8\" spark plug socket with a rubber insert to hold the plug, and a short extension for clearance under the hood. Apply a small dab of anti-seize on the threads (a thin film, not a glob \u2014 too much and the torque reading gets thrown off). Thread by hand for at least three turns to confirm you haven't cross-threaded the aluminum head \u2014 this is the most common failure point for a plug change. Torque to **27 ft-lbs**.\n\nWhile you're in there, look at each old plug as it comes out. White ash = lean, oily black = rich or burning oil, dry black = running rich or short trips. A normal plug pulled from a healthy 4.0L will be tan to light brown. If one plug looks different from the other five, that cylinder has a problem worth chasing.","steps":["Cold engine. Pull #1 plug wire (front of engine), twisting at the boot before pulling straight up.","Blow debris out of the plug well with compressed air or by mouth.","Use 5/8\" spark plug socket with extension to break the plug loose; spin out by hand.","Inspect old plug; note color and condition.","Set new plug gap to 0.035\" with feeler gauge.","Apply thin film of anti-seize to plug threads.","Hand-thread new plug at least 3 turns before tools.","Torque to 27 ft-lbs.","Push wire boot fully onto plug terminal until it clicks.","Repeat for plugs 2 through 6, one at a time."],"pros":["Straight-line plug layout means every plug is reachable without removing intake or accessories.","$18 set of copper Champions lasts 30,000 miles in normal driving.","Good diagnostic signal \u2014 color of old plugs tells you what each cylinder is doing."],"cons":["Anti-seize gets debated forever online \u2014 use a thin film; don't skip it, don't over-apply.","Cross-threading an aluminum head is a several-hundred-dollar repair; always start by hand.","Plug wires aren't included; pair this with a wire-set replacement every other plug change."]},{"id":"yj-transfer-case-fluid","title":"Transfer Case Fluid \u2014 NP207 and NP231","vehicle":"yj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"fluids","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":35},"tools_required":["3/8\" drive ratchet (square plug)","drain pan","fluid pump or squeeze bottle","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["fluids","drivetrain","transfer-case","np207","np231"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline DEX/MERC ATF, 1 qt","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Mopar ATF+4, 1 qt","part_number":"68218057AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Drain/fill plug crush washer","vendor":"Dorman","price_estimate_usd":3}],"citations":[{"title":"NP231 fluid type and capacity \u2014 Wrangler Forum","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/what-fluid-and-quantity-for-np231-transfer-case.1284833/"},{"title":"NP-231 transfer case fluid \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/transfer-case-np-231-fluid-question.1273653/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Both YJ transfer cases \u2014 the NP207 (1987\u20131988) and the NP231 (1988\u20131995) \u2014 use ATF, not gear oil. Drain plug bottom, fill plug side, 2.2 quarts on the NP207, 2.2 quarts on the NP231. The whole job takes about 30 minutes.","description":"The single most common YJ transfer case mistake is dumping 80W-90 into it because it lives next to the differentials that *do* take gear oil. The NP207 and NP231 are both chain-driven, with an internal oil pump that depends on the lighter weight of automatic transmission fluid to circulate. Gear oil starves the pump, the chain wears, and you find out about it when the case starts whining in 4-high. Use ATF.\n\nEither Dexron/Mercon ATF or ATF+4 is acceptable in the NP231 \u2014 the chain and synchros aren't fussy. ATF+4 is the modern Mopar spec; Dexron VI works fine and costs less. The earlier NP207 (only in 1987 and very early 1988) was originally spec'd for Dexron II. Use any current Dexron-compatible ATF and you're covered. Capacity for both is roughly 2.2 quarts \u2014 but always fill to the **bottom of the fill plug** rather than measuring; that's what \"full\" means on a transfer case.\n\nThe drain and fill plugs on the NP231 are 3/8-inch square \u2014 your 3/8-inch ratchet head fits directly into the plug with no socket. The NP207 uses the same square pattern. Crack the **fill plug first** before draining; if you can't get the fill plug out, you can't refill the case, and you do not want that discovery after the case is empty. Once the fill plug breaks loose, remove the drain plug and let it drain into a pan. Dark, smelly fluid is normal. Metal flakes on the magnetic drain plug are normal. Big chunks of metal are not \u2014 that's a sign of internal wear and a case rebuild is on the horizon.\n\nRefill until ATF dribbles out of the fill hole, reinstall both plugs with fresh crush washers, torque to **25 ft-lbs**, and drive it. Cycle through 2H, 4H, and 4L on a quiet street and listen \u2014 a healthy case is nearly silent. Whining, clunking, or a hard 4L engagement points at chain stretch or worn synchros.","steps":["Park level, warm the transfer case (drive 5 minutes), park, and chock the wheels.","Locate fill plug on the driver-side rear of the case; crack it loose first \u2014 do not remove yet.","Position drain pan under the lowest point of the case.","Remove drain plug (3/8-inch square); let fully drain (10\u201315 minutes).","Inspect the magnetic drain plug \u2014 light fuzz is normal; chunks mean trouble.","Reinstall drain plug with fresh crush washer; torque to 25 ft-lbs.","Pump ATF into fill hole until it dribbles out of the threads.","Reinstall fill plug; torque to 25 ft-lbs.","Test in 2H, 4H, and 4L; check for leaks at both plugs after 24 hours."],"pros":["30-minute job with hand tools \u2014 no jack stands required if you can reach under the case.","Square-plug design means no rounded sockets, no stripped Allen heads.","Doing this every 30,000 miles keeps the chain and pump healthy."],"cons":["ATF dribbles down the crossmember every time \u2014 keep paper towels staged.","The fill plug seizes on old jeeps; soak it with PB Blaster the night before.","Commonly confused the transfer case with the transmission below it \u2014 verify you're at the rear case before draining."]},{"id":"recovery-gear-yj","title":"Recovery Gear Kit for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery_kit","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":500},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["recovery techniques","strap attachment safety"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["recovery","straps","shackles","hi-lift","traction-boards","safety","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Snatch Strap 30' / 20,000 lb rating","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Bubba Rope 3/4\" D-ring shackles (2-pack)","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Hi-Lift Jack 48\"","vendor":"Hi-Lift","price_estimate_usd":85},{"name":"MAXTRAX MKII traction boards (pair)","vendor":"MAXTRAX","price_estimate_usd":290}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB recovery gear guide","url":"https://arb.com.au/recovery"},{"title":"Hi-Lift Jack use guide","url":"https://www.hi-lift.com/"}],"alternatives":["winch-yj","winch-wiring-yj","front-bumper-yj","rear-bumper-yj"],"summary":"Recovery gear is what gets you unstuck before you call for help \u2014 and a YJ at 3,400 lbs stock weight is light enough that a proper snatch strap recovery with a buddy is usually fast and drama-free. Size the equipment to the vehicle and know your recovery attachment points before you need them.","description":"The YJ's factory tow hooks are a rated forward pull point \u2014 they're fine for a straight-ahead strap recovery by another vehicle. They are not designed for side-loading, for lateral yank recoveries, or for winch attachment. For anything other than a simple forward pull, attach to a rated D-ring mount on a steel bumper.\n\nFor a stock or mildly loaded YJ at 3,400 lbs, a 20,000 lb rated snatch strap provides a 6x safety margin \u2014 appropriate given the peak loads during a kinetic recovery. ARB's 30-foot strap is the standard recommendation: it's long enough to give the recovery vehicle room to build momentum, rated appropriately, and well-made. A tow strap with no elasticity is not a recovery strap; it transfers shock load without the kinetic energy absorption that makes snatch recoveries effective.\n\nTwo 3/4-inch bow shackles at minimum. Confirm the WLL (Working Load Limit) is stamped on the shackle. Back the pin off a quarter turn after tightening so it doesn't self-lock under load and become unremovable. Replace any shackle that has been shock-loaded or shows deformation.\n\nA Hi-Lift Jack is appropriate on a YJ in a way it isn't on every vehicle \u2014 the YJ has body structure you can actually lift from. With a bumper guard or Hi-Lift-compatible receiver, the jack lets you lift a corner, reposition a tire out of a rut, or pull a door open after a hard impact. Carry it mounted outside the cab; inside, it's a hazard in a rollover. Learn to use it before you need it.\n\nFactory tow hooks: forward straight-pull only. Steel front bumper D-rings: full recovery use. Steel rear bumper D-rings: full recovery use. Frame rails looped with a tree saver strap: full recovery use. Ball hitch: NEVER a recovery point under any circumstances \u2014 the ball can shear under recovery load and become a projectile."},{"id":"winch-yj","title":"Winch Selection for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"recovery","subcategory":"winch","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":550,"high":1200},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["recovery","winch","synthetic-rope","bumper","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn VR10000-S (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":800},{"name":"Superwinch Talon 9.5i (9,500 lb)","vendor":"Superwinch","price_estimate_usd":550},{"name":"Warn M8274-50 (8,274 lb, above-bumper mount)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1100}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn VR10000-S product page","url":"https://www.warn.com/vr10000s"},{"title":"Warn M8274-50 winch","url":"https://www.warn.com/m8274-50"}],"alternatives":["winch-wiring-yj","front-bumper-yj","recovery-gear-yj"],"summary":"The YJ is one of the lighter Wranglers \u2014 at 3,400 lbs stock, a 9,500\u201310,000 lb winch provides nearly a 3x rated capacity margin over vehicle weight. A 12,000-lb winch is not wrong, but it's more than needed for the platform and adds cost and weight. The 10,000-lb tier is the right specification for a YJ.","description":"Before the winch goes on, the bumper needs to be winch-compatible and properly rated. A winch bolted to the factory plastic bumper is not a safe recovery setup. Confirm the front bumper's winch plate is rated for the winch you're installing.\n\nThe Warn VR10000-S is a sealed, synthetic rope winch at the 10,000-lb class. The VR series uses an efficient motor and a reliable solenoid. The synthetic rope is safer than steel when it parts under load. It's the right modern choice for a YJ that does regular trail use, priced at $800. Like all Warn products, it's field-serviceable and parts are available.\n\nAt $550 the Talon 9.5i is the value pick. It's a 9,500-lb winch with synthetic rope, adequate motor and solenoid, and it works. The Superwinch is less refined in build quality compared to Warn but functions correctly. For a YJ on a budget where money is better spent on other components, the Talon 9.5i is a legitimate choice.\n\nThe M8274-50 is an above-bumper mount winch with a legendary reputation. It mounts higher on the bumper rather than recessed in a winch plate, which keeps the mechanism out of water during crossings. It's mechanically different from typical worm-gear winches \u2014 faster line speed, different gear design \u2014 and is entirely rebuildable. At $1,100 it's the expensive option, but for a long-term dedicated trail YJ it's a compelling choice. Requires a bumper designed for above-bumper mounting.\n\nThe YJ's front bumper sits relatively low, which means the winch line to an anchor point (tree, anchor strap, another vehicle) is often at a slight upward angle \u2014 favorable for straight-line pulls. Avoid extreme downward angles that stress the winch drum flange. If the anchor is significantly lower than the bumper, use a pulley block to improve the line angle."},{"id":"yj-tie-rods","title":"Tie Rod and Drag Link Replacement \u2014 YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"steering","subcategory":"linkage","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack + jack stands","tie-rod separator / pickle fork","large wrenches","torque wrench","tape measure (toe)","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["steering linkage work","setting toe to drive to an alignment"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["steering","tie-rod","drag-link","wander","death-wobble","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Tie rod assembly with new ends (stock or HD)","vendor":"Crown / Moog / Synergy","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Drag link assembly with ends","vendor":"Crown / Moog","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Castle nuts + new cotter pins (hardware)","vendor":"Mopar / hardware","price_estimate_usd":10}],"citations":[{"title":"Moog steering and suspension \u2014 Jeep YJ tie rod / drag link","url":"https://www.moogparts.com/"},{"title":"Jeep YJ FSM \u2014 steering linkage service and torque","url":"https://www.jeep.com/"}],"alternatives":["yj-steering-wander-wobble","yj-dana-30-front"],"summary":"Worn tie rod and drag link ends are a top cause of YJ steering wander and a major contributor to front-end wobble \u2014 and because they are part of the steering, a failed end means losing steering control. The YJ's linkage is uncomplicated and cheap to replace, and fresh ends transform a vague, darty Jeep into one that tracks straight. Budget $120-$400 depending on stock versus heavy-duty parts, and finish with a professional alignment. You can set toe well enough to drive home, but the Jeep needs a real alignment to wear tires evenly and steer right.","description":"The YJ steers its solid Dana 30 front axle through a tie rod (linking the two wheels) and a drag link (from the steering box to the knuckle). Both use tapered ball-stud ends that wear out with age and trail miles. Loose linkage shows up as on-center wander, a clunk when you change direction, and feathered front tire wear \u2014 and on a leaf-sprung short-wheelbase Jeep, worn ends feed directly into the front-end wobble these trucks are prone to.\n\nLift the front and grab each tire at 3-and-9 o'clock, pushing and pulling with the steering locked: play there points at a tie rod end. Have a helper rock the steering wheel while you watch the drag link and tie rod ends for any lash or a torn boot. A boot that is split has been letting in grit and water, so that joint is worn whether or not it rattles yet. Separate this from ball-joint and wheel-bearing play (felt at 12-and-6) so you fix the right part.\n\nSoak the castle nuts and tapers in penetrating oil. Remove the cotter pin and castle nut, then separate the taper with a tie-rod separator (a pickle fork works but tears the boot, so only fork a part you are replacing). Before removing the old assembly, measure its length or count exposed threads so the new part goes back at the same length, preserving toe well enough to drive. Torque each stud to the service-manual value and install **new** cotter pins \u2014 a steering fastener without its cotter pin is a failure waiting to happen.\n\nSet toe close with a tape measure (compare the front-of-tire and rear-of-tire distances) so the Jeep tracks straight, then get a professional alignment to dial toe and verify caster. Correct caster is what gives a solid-axle Jeep its on-center stability and resistance to wobble, so do not skip it \u2014 especially on a lifted YJ where lift changes caster. Re-check the fasteners after a few hundred miles, and treat any new looseness or a torn boot as a near-term safety repair.","pros":["Restores straight, predictable steering and stops edge tire wear","Cheap, uncomplicated linkage \u2014 a high-value safety repair for the money","Removes a primary contributor to YJ front-end wobble"],"cons":["A failed steering end is a genuine safety hazard \u2014 do not defer it","Separating tapers cleanly needs the right tool to save good boots","A home toe-set gets you driving; a real alignment is non-negotiable"]},{"id":"yj-steering-wander-wobble","title":"Steering Wander and Front-End Wobble","vehicle":"yj","category":"steering","subcategory":"diagnostics","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","pry bar","13-21mm sockets and wrenches","grease gun"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","methodical diagnosis"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["steering","safety-critical","death-wobble"],"parts":[{"name":"Greasable tie-rod ends (set)","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Steering box (remanufactured)","vendor":"Lares/Cardone","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Steering stabilizer","vendor":"Bilstein/Old Man Emu","price_estimate_usd":70}],"citations":[{"title":"NAXJA / YJ steering tech threads","url":"https://naxja.org/"},{"title":"Moog YJ steering catalog","url":"https://www.moog-suspension-parts.com/"}],"alternatives":["yj-lift-kit-guide"],"summary":"A leaf-sprung YJ does not get the violent track-bar death wobble that coil Jeeps do \u2014 it has no front track bar. What it gets is steering wander, a loose on-center feel, and a shimmy over washboard, and the cause is almost always worn tie-rod ends, a tired steering box, or out-of-balance tires. Check those three before you spend a dollar.","description":"The honest answer: most YJ steering complaints trace to slop in the linkage, not the suspension. With the front wheels off the ground, grab each front tire at 9 and 3 and push-pull \u2014 play there is a tie-rod end or the steering box sector shaft. Then grab at 12 and 6 \u2014 play there is a wheel bearing or ball joint. A helper turning the wheel while you watch the steering box pitman arm will show sector-shaft lash; the YJ box has an adjustment screw on top, but if it needs more than a small nudge the box is worn out and a reman unit is the real fix.\n\nTires matter more than YJ owners expect. A single out-of-round or unbalanced 31\u201333\" tire will set up a shimmy that feels like a front-end problem. Rebalance (and road-force balance if a shop has the machine) before condemning parts. Worn leaf-spring bushings and loose spring shackles also let the axle walk under braking \u2014 check the shackle bolts and the bushing eyes.\n\nA steering stabilizer is a damper, not a cure. It will quiet a mild shimmy, but if you are relying on it to mask wander, you are hiding a worn part that will eventually let go. Fix the linkage first, then add a quality stabilizer for the last 10 percent.","steps":["Lift the front, support on jack stands under the frame, and check linkage play at 9-and-3 and ball joints/bearings at 12-and-6.","Inspect the drag link and tie-rod ends for torn boots and movement; replace any with play.","Check steering box mounting bolts and sector-shaft lash; adjust lightly or replace a worn box.","Rebalance the front tires (road-force if possible) and inspect leaf bushings and shackle bolts.","Only after the above, fit a quality steering stabilizer."]},{"id":"yj-leaf-spring-service","title":"YJ Front and Rear Leaf Spring Service","vehicle":"yj","category":"suspension","subcategory":"leaf-springs","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands (rated 3 tons)","18mm/19mm sockets","21mm socket","breaker bar","torque wrench (up to 150 ft-lbs)","spring spreader or pry bar"],"skills_required":["intermediate suspension work","comfortable with rusty fasteners"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["suspension","leaf-springs","u-bolts","ride-quality"],"parts":[{"name":"Old Man Emu stock-height YJ front leaf pack","part_number":"CS037R","vendor":"ARB/OME","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Rough Country 2.5\" lift YJ leaf pack (front pair)","part_number":"8023","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"U-bolt kit (front pair, fits stock-height YJ)","vendor":"Daystar/Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Greaseable bolt and bushing kit","vendor":"Currie/Daystar","price_estimate_usd":65}],"citations":[{"title":"Wrangler YJ Suspension Torque Specs \u2014 ExtremeTerrain","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/wrangler-yj-suspension-torque-specs.html"},{"title":"YJ leaf spring torque thread \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/actual-leaf-spring-and-u-bolt-torque-specs.1366033/"}],"alternatives":["body-lift-vs-spring","shocks-yj"],"summary":"A YJ leaf pack lasts 100,000 miles if you're lucky, less if it's lived in the rust belt. Sagging springs, broken main leaves, and snapped center pins all warrant replacement. Plan on a full day for both axles, half a day for one. Torque the u-bolts to 90 ft-lbs; everything else gets torqued with the wheels on the ground.","description":"The YJ runs leaf springs front and rear \u2014 five-leaf packs that double as both spring and axle locator. When they sag, ride height drops, caster angle drifts out of spec, and steering goes vague. When the center pin snaps, the axle walks rearward on the spring and your driveshaft angles change in a hurry. Bushings dry out and squeak. If your YJ has 150,000 miles or has lived through more than two desert summers, the springs are probably tired.\n\nStock-height replacements from Old Man Emu (OME) or BDS will give you back factory ride height with fresh bushings and a controlled rate. If you've been planning a 2.5-inch or 4-inch lift, do the springs and the lift in the same job \u2014 replacing leaves twice in a year is wasted work. Rough Country, Skyjacker, and BDS all make YJ-specific kits. Avoid mystery aftermarket \"lift springs\" from auction sites; the steel quality and arch consistency between pairs is unpredictable.\n\nThe factory torque spec is **u-bolts: 90 ft-lbs, shackle bolts: 95 ft-lbs, main eye bolts: 105 ft-lbs, crossmember bolts: 30 ft-lbs**. Some installers torque u-bolts to 70\u201380 ft-lbs out of fear of bending the spring plates \u2014 the factory plates are stamped steel and the 90 ft-lbs spec assumes them. Heavier aftermarket plates handle the full 90 ft-lbs with no complaint. **U-bolts can be torqued at any time. Shackle bolts and main eye bolts must be torqued with the full weight of the vehicle on the ground**, or the bushing seats compress to one position and bind through suspension travel.\n\nThe hardest part of the job is breaking loose 30-year-old shackle bolts. Soak every bolt with PB Blaster the night before, then heat with a torch if needed. Cutting the bolts and replacing with greaseable hardware is often faster than fighting them out intact. Greaseable bolts also make future bushing service trivial. Plan for a $40 hardware budget on top of the spring cost.","steps":["Soak every shackle and main eye bolt with penetrating oil the day before.","Park level, chock the wheels, raise the frame and support on jack stands.","Support the axle with a floor jack \u2014 do not let it hang from brake lines.","Remove the lower shock mount.","Remove u-bolts (4 nuts per spring, 21mm typical).","Remove shackle bolts (rear of spring) and main eye bolt (front of spring).","Pull the spring; transfer the center-pin alignment to the new spring if needed.","Install the new spring: main eye first, then shackle, then position the axle on the spring pin and snug u-bolts.","Torque u-bolts to 90 ft-lbs (or per spring manufacturer's spec for aftermarket plates).","Reinstall the shock; lower the truck to the ground.","With full weight on the wheels, torque shackle bolts to 95 ft-lbs and main eye bolts to 105 ft-lbs.","Recheck all u-bolt torques after 500 miles \u2014 new springs settle and u-bolts loosen."],"pros":["Factory ride height and predictable handling come back overnight.","Greaseable hardware ends the squeak-and-replace cycle for the rest of the truck's life.","Single-job opportunity to upgrade lift height if you've been considering it."],"cons":["Rusty shackle bolts can turn a half-day job into a two-day fight.","Cheap aftermarket springs vary 1/2-inch side-to-side in arch \u2014 measure the new pair before installation.","Suspension geometry shifts will throw caster out of spec; budget for a degreed shim kit if you lift."]},{"id":"yj-shocks","title":"Shock Replacement \u2014 Stock and Lift Heights","vehicle":"yj","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":350},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","18mm and 21mm sockets/wrenches","vise grips (for stuck shock studs)","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["suspension","shocks","bilstein","ride-quality"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 4600 stock-height shock (front, YJ)","part_number":"24-014434","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Bilstein 4600 stock-height shock (rear, YJ)","part_number":"24-185486","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Bilstein 5100 3\u20134\" lift front shock (YJ)","part_number":"24-064576","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Rancho RS5000X 2.5\" lift shock set (YJ)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":220}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 5100 3\u20134\" YJ front shock \u2014 ExtremeTerrain","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/bilstein-5100-series-f-34-yj-24-064576.html"},{"title":"Bilstein 5100 3\u20134\" YJ shock kit \u2014 suspensionlifts.com","url":"https://www.suspensionlifts.com/shop/jeep/wrangler-yj/86-95-jeep-wrangler-yj-4wd-bilstein-5100-3-4-front-3-4-rear-lift-shocks/"}],"alternatives":["leaf-spring-service-yj"],"summary":"Stock-height YJ runs Bilstein 4600 or Rancho RS5000 in factory length. A 2.5-inch lift runs a slightly longer shock; a 3\u20134-inch lift wants the Bilstein 5100 with 28.44-inch extended / 16.97-inch collapsed travel. Match shock length to lift height \u2014 too short bottoms the shaft, too long limits droop.","description":"The YJ uses four shocks \u2014 eye-loop on the bottom and a stem with two nuts on the top. They're straightforward to access, straightforward to swap, and probably worn out on any YJ that hasn't had them replaced in the last 80,000 miles. A worn shock won't pass a \"park-the-truck and shove the corner downward\" bounce test: if the truck oscillates more than one and a half times before settling, the shock is shot.\n\nBilstein 4600 monotube is the safe default for a stock-height YJ. Bilstein 5100 is the same valving in a longer body for lifted trucks and gives a noticeable improvement in trail manners. Rancho RS5000X is the budget alternative \u2014 softer, more comfortable on-road, less composed at speed. The expensive options (Bilstein 7100, King 2.0, Fox 2.0) only make sense on a built rig that sees triple-digit trail speed; on a daily-driven YJ they're overkill.\n\nLength is the part nobody gets right. A stock YJ front shock is **24.5-inch extended, 14.5-inch collapsed**. A 2.5-inch lift wants roughly **27-inch extended, 16-inch collapsed**. A 4-inch lift wants **28.4-inch extended, 17-inch collapsed** \u2014 that's the Bilstein 5100 24-064576. Run a shock that's too short and you'll bottom the shaft on every long droop, which destroys the seal and dumps oil within months. Run too long and you cap suspension travel before the axle is at full extension. Measure your current suspension at full droop (let it hang on the springs only) and order shocks rated for that extended length plus an inch of margin.\n\nThe eye-loop bushings deserve a fresh squirt of silicone or rubber lube before final torque. The stem-style upper mount has two nuts \u2014 torque the bottom (inner) nut against the shock stem to **35 ft-lbs**, then snug the locking nut on top. Don't over-torque the eye bolts; the rubber bushings should compress only slightly when you tighten, not deform into the metal sleeve.","steps":["Park level, chock wheels, raise the frame, and support on jack stands.","Support the axle with a floor jack to maintain ride height at the eye end.","Remove the upper stem nuts (front uppers are 13mm; rear uppers are 18mm typically).","Remove the lower eye-loop bolt (18mm or 21mm depending on year).","Pull old shock out; inspect bushings on both ends.","Install new shock, upper stem first; thread on inner mount nut by hand.","Insert lower eye-loop bolt with fresh bushings; torque to 50 ft-lbs.","Torque upper stem mount nut to 35 ft-lbs; snug locking nut.","Lower the truck and bounce-test each corner \u2014 one and a half oscillations max."],"pros":["One of the highest-impact suspension upgrades for the dollar \u2014 $300 of shocks transforms a tired YJ.","Eye/stem layout makes the job tool-light: a couple of wrenches and a floor jack.","Can step up later (4600 \u2192 5100 \u2192 reservoir shock) without changing mounts."],"cons":["Frozen upper stem nuts spin the shaft instead of loosening \u2014 vise grips on the flat are mandatory.","Wrong-length shock will limit articulation or destroy itself on droop; measure before ordering.","Replacing only the worn pair (front or rear) gives an unbalanced ride \u2014 do all four together."]},{"id":"yj-lift-kit-guide","title":"Lift Kit Guide: Springs, Shackles, and Spring-Over","vehicle":"yj","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","spring compressor","breaker bar","torque wrench","angle finder"],"skills_required":["wrenching","suspension geometry basics","brake-line awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1990","1991-1995"],"tags":["suspension","lift","tires"],"parts":[{"name":"Full leaf-spring lift kit (2.5-4 in)","vendor":"Rough Country/Skyjacker/Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":600},{"name":"Greasable shackles + bushings","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Extended brake lines (front + rear)","vendor":"Crown/Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Adjustable track bar (rear)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":120}],"citations":[{"title":"Rubicon Express YJ application guide","url":"https://www.rubiconexpress.com/"},{"title":"Quadratec YJ suspension catalog","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/vehicle/1987-1995-wrangler-yj/suspension"}],"alternatives":["yj-steering-wander-wobble","yj-dana-35-rear-upgrade"],"summary":"The YJ is leaf-sprung at both ends, which makes lifting it cheaper than any coil Jeep and the geometry forgiving. A 2.5\u20134 inch spring kit clears 31\u201333s and bolts in over a weekend. Go past that and a spring-over axle (SOA) is the honest path to 35s \u2014 not taller shackles.","description":"Verdict first: full-replacement leaf springs are worth the extra money over add-a-leaf or lift shackles. Add-a-leafs stiffen an already-stiff ride and sag within a year; long shackles change your spring angle and make the truck wander. A quality 2.5\u20134\" spring pack from Rubicon Express, Skyjacker, or Rough Country gives you real travel, predictable handling, and room for 31\u201333\" tires on the stock Dana 30/35 axles.\n\nWhatever the height, budget for the supporting parts. Extended front and rear brake lines are mandatory above 2.5\" \u2014 stock lines pull tight at full droop and split. A longer or adjustable rear track bar re-centers the axle. Above ~3.5\", you are into driveline vibration territory: the rear pinion angle needs shims, and a slip-yoke-eliminator with a CV driveshaft is the proper cure on a lifted YJ, not a transfer-case drop.\n\nFor 35s, stop adding lift on top of leaf springs. A spring-over conversion \u2014 relocating the springs above the axle instead of below \u2014 nets roughly 5\u20136 inches with flex no shackle lift can match, but it requires new spring perches, a steering correction, and an SYE/CV driveshaft. It is a real fabrication job; plan it as one, or run 33s and call it done.","steps":["Lift, support the frame, and remove the wheels; let the axles hang to unload the springs.","Replace springs one corner at a time so the axle stays located; torque U-bolts to spec.","Install extended brake lines and an adjustable rear track bar.","Set rear pinion angle with shims; add an SYE/CV shaft above ~3.5 in.","Re-torque everything after a shakedown drive and re-check alignment (caster/toe)."]},{"id":"diagnostics-yj","title":"Diagnosing the YJ \u2014 OBD1 and RENIX Era","vehicle":"yj","category":"tools_diagnostic","subcategory":"diagnostics","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":200},"tools_required":["ignition key (for flash codes)","DRBIII or compatible scan tool (1991+ optional)"],"skills_required":["reading fault codes","basic electrical diagnosis"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["tools_diagnostic","diagnostics","obd1","renix","fault-codes","check-engine","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"OTC 3421 DRBIII-compatible scan tool (1991-1995 YJ)","vendor":"OTC","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep YJ RENIX diagnostics \u2014 JeepForum.com","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f11/renix-diagnostics/"},{"title":"YJ OBD1 flash codes reference \u2014 WranglerForum.com","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/f195/yj-diagnostic-codes/"}],"alternatives":["hard-start-no-start-yj"],"summary":"The YJ is a pre-OBD2 vehicle from start to finish \u2014 the OBD2 standard didn't arrive until 1996 model year. Every YJ from 1987 to 1995 uses a simpler diagnostic system where fault codes are read by watching the check engine light flash a sequence of numbers. No scan tool is required for basic fault code retrieval; you need your ignition key and 30 seconds of attention.","description":"The two eras of YJ diagnostics divide at 1991: the 1987\u20131990 RENIX (Renault-Bendix) fuel injection system and the 1991\u20131995 Chrysler-era injection system. Both use flash codes read the same way, but the code numbers and fault definitions differ, and the 1991+ system can also be read with a DRBIII-compatible scan tool for live sensor data.\n\nTurn the ignition key to ON-OFF-ON-OFF-ON within 5 seconds (do not start the engine \u2014 key to ON position only, then back to OFF, three times ending in ON). The check engine light will flash a sequence of numbers. Count the flashes: a short pause separates the digits of each code, and a longer pause separates codes. Code 55 indicates end of sequence (5 flashes, pause, 5 flashes). Write down all codes before the sequence repeats.\n\nThis procedure works on all 1987\u20131995 YJs. It requires no tools. Every YJ owner should memorize this sequence.\n\nThe RENIX system on 1987\u20131990 4.2L YJs has its own code set. The most common faults:\n- **Code 12**: Battery was disconnected within the last 50 key cycles \u2014 informational, not a fault\n- **Code 27**: Injector control circuit fault \u2014 check wiring to injectors\n- **Code 33**: MAP (manifold absolute pressure) sensor signal out of range \u2014 sensor or wiring\n- **Code 43**: Ignition coil control circuit \u2014 coil or driver circuit\n- **Code 44**: Battery temperature sensor \u2014 common on high-mileage RENIX systems\n- **Code 51**: O2 sensor signal fault \u2014 O2 sensor or exhaust leak upstream of sensor\n- **Code 53**: Internal controller failure \u2014 Chrysler PCM failure, serious\n\nThe 1991\u20131995 YJ uses the same flash code protocol but the Chrysler-era code set:\n- **Code 12**: Battery was disconnected \u2014 informational\n- **Code 21**: Oxygen sensor signal out of range\n- **Code 22**: Coolant temperature sensor \u2014 common fault on aging sensors\n- **Code 24**: Throttle position sensor out of range\n- **Code 27**: Fuel injector control\n- **Code 33**: Air conditioning clutch relay circuit (even if AC deleted)\n- **Code 43**: Peak primary coil current not achieved\n- **Code 53**: Internal PCM failure\n\nThe 1991\u20131995 YJ uses the Chrysler SCI (Serial Communication Interface) protocol, which a DRBIII-compatible scan tool can read for live sensor data: coolant temp, MAP sensor voltage, TPS voltage, O2 sensor cross-counts, and more. This is significantly more useful than flash codes alone when diagnosing intermittent problems or sensor drift. The OTC 3421 is a commonly available compatible tool. Generic OBD2 scan tools from an auto parts store will NOT work on a YJ \u2014 they require OBD2, and the YJ is OBD1.\n\nThe 1987\u20131990 RENIX system has a reputation for reliability problems with the crank position sensor and the MAP sensor. If a RENIX YJ is hard-starting, stumbling at idle, or cutting out under load, check the crank sensor wiring harness at the bellhousing \u2014 it chafes on the transmission and causes intermittent no-start faults that won't always throw a persistent code. The RENIX diagnostic community on JeepForum.com maintains detailed guides that supplement the factory FSM."},{"id":"tire-sizing-yj","title":"Tire Fitment Guide for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tires","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["wheels_tires","tires","fitment","lift","33s","leaf-springs","yj"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"YJ Wrangler specifications \u2014 CJ Pony Parts","url":"https://www.cjponyparts.com/resources/yj-wrangler-specs"},{"title":"Pirate4x4 YJ tire and lift guide","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/"}],"alternatives":["yj-leaf-spring-service","wheels-yj"],"summary":"The YJ uses leaf springs front and rear, which changes the lift-and-tire equation compared to the coil-sprung TJ. Leaf-spring lift is cheaper: longer shackles and replacement leaf packs cost significantly less than coil kits. The tradeoff is that the leaf-spring geometry requires more attention to body clearance at the rear, and wider tires will rub the rear tub in ways that coil-sprung vehicles don't experience in the same spots.","description":"Tire sizing on the YJ follows a leaf-spring-specific logic. The front clears tires reasonably predictably with fender trimming. The rear is where the leaf-spring arch and the tire path during suspension travel intersects with the body \u2014 wider tires need more body trimming at the rear.\n\nFactory YJ sits on 30-inch tires. Stock clearance is adequate for 30s. No modifications needed.\n\nA shackle lift and/or longer leaf springs at 2 inches gets you to 31s. Cost: $80\u2013250 for shackles, more for replacement leaf packs. Minor front fender liner trimming at full lock is typical. Rear clearance is usually adequate at 31s without body trimming.\n\nA 4-inch leaf lift \u2014 replacement leaf packs, longer shackles, and quality shocks \u2014 clears 33x12.5R15 tires. This is the YJ sweet spot: affordable lift, meaningful capability gain, manageable tire cost. The rear tub typically needs trimming at the top rear of the wheel opening to clear a 33x12.5 at full compression. The front fender liner needs trimming at full lock. A regear is strongly recommended (4.56 target) to maintain usable performance.\n\nA spring-over axle (SOA) conversion or an aggressive long-arm lift in the 5-inch range can accommodate 35s on a dedicated rock crawler YJ. This is less common and generally reserved for builds that prioritize trail capability over road manners. At 35s, a regear to 4.88 or 5.13 is required, a front axle plan is needed (the Dana 30 is marginal at 35s with a locker), and extensive body trimming is necessary.\n\nOn the YJ, 33s with factory 3.55 gears is noticeably soft but functional. Regearing to 4.56 restores proper power delivery on 33s and the YJ drives like a different vehicle. With 35s, 4.88 is the minimum; 5.13 pairs well with the AX-15 manual's gear ratios. Do not skip the regear \u2014 it's the single biggest usable-performance improvement in a tire-and-lift build."},{"id":"wheels-yj","title":"Wheel Selection for the YJ","vehicle":"yj","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":375,"high":1000},"tools_required":["torque wrench","breaker bar or impact"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1987-1995"],"tags":["wheels_tires","wheels","backspacing","bolt-pattern","fitment","yj"],"parts":[{"name":"Pro Comp 52 Steel Wheel 15x8 (each)","vendor":"Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Mickey Thompson Classic II 15x8 (each)","vendor":"Mickey Thompson","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Method 305 NV 17x9 (each)","vendor":"Method","price_estimate_usd":220}],"citations":[{"title":"Pro Comp steel wheel guide","url":"https://www.procompusa.com/"},{"title":"Method 305 NV specs","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com/305-nv"}],"alternatives":["tire-sizing-yj"],"summary":"The YJ uses the same 5x4.5-inch (5x114.3mm) bolt pattern as every TJ, LJ, and JK Wrangler built through 2018. A wheel that fits a TJ fits a YJ, which opens the full Wrangler aftermarket. The meaningful variables are wheel size (15 versus 17 inch), width, and backspacing. Get backspacing right and the wheel is invisible; get it wrong and you either rub or have the tires sticking out past the flares.","description":"Steel 15-inch wheels are the correct choice for a trail YJ. Affordable, replaceable, and they bend where alloy wheels crack. A cracked alloy wheel on trail is often unserviceable; a bent steel wheel can often be hammered back into usability for the drive out.\n\nPro Comp Series 52 and Mickey Thompson Classic II are the benchmark steel wheels for the YJ. Both are 15x8 inches, available in 3.75 and 4.25-inch backspacing, and priced at $75\u201390 each. A set of five runs $375\u2013450. For any YJ that sees trail use regularly, this is the correct wheel.\n\nFor 33x12.5R15 on a 4-inch lift: 3.75-inch backspacing positions the tire at the right clearance point for the front and rear fender openings. Running 4.25-inch backspacing with 33s tucks the tire in slightly \u2014 sometimes needed to clear the rear leaf spring packs at full compression. Measure the specific clearance for your setup; the leaf-spring geometry on the YJ creates different clearance relationships than the coil-sprung TJ.\n\nMethod 305 NV at 17x9 inches is the quality alloy choice that works with 33 and 35-inch tires. At $220/each the cost is real, and the YJ's leaf springs and older suspension components will stress the alloy more than a modern coil-sprung Wrangler. For a street-trail YJ where appearance matters, the 17-inch alloy is worth it. For a dedicated rock crawler, stick with 15-inch steel.\n\nBeadlock wheels for a YJ are overkill unless the rig is a dedicated rock crawler running tire pressures below 10 PSI regularly. For trail use at 15\u201320 PSI, beadlocks add cost, weight, and complexity with no meaningful benefit. Skip them on a trail YJ."}];